const MOVIES_DISNEY = [{"num": 253, "t": "Pinocchio", "y": "1940", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "CSM calls this Disney masterpiece \"darker than you may remember,\" recommending it for about age 6+. Concerns: Pinocchio is kidnapped, caged, and threatened with destruction, boys get into fights and destroy property, and a character fires a gun. Most startling to modern parents, Pinocchio gets drunk on beer and smokes cigars during the Pleasure Island sequence. Positive: Jiminy Cricket models conscience and guidance, Geppetto shows selfless love, and the story explores honesty, courage, and unselfishness through real consequences.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 254, "t": "Fantasia", "y": "1940", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Breathtaking animation feat -- with some creepy visuals.\" Recommended for about age 6+. Concerns: the pairing of dark, ominous music with unsettling imagery -- flooding caused by out-of-control broomsticks, dinosaurs locked in deadly battle, and a finale full of skeletons, graves, and ghosts -- can frighten young or sensitive kids; mythological centaurs drink wine and one deity gets tipsy. Positive: music, color, and movement are shown as deeply connected, and Mickey Mouse's Sorcerer's Apprentice segment models curiosity, courage, and the consequences of overreaching.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 255, "t": "Dumbo", "y": "1941", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Sweet Disney classic is still lovely, despite stereotypes.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: cruel circus kids torment Dumbo, his mother is imprisoned and separated from him after defending him, and the collapsing circus tent is a genuinely scary set piece; silhouetted clowns are shown drinking, and Dumbo and Timothy get accidentally drunk, triggering the trippy \"Pink Elephants on Parade\" sequence. CSM also flags the crow characters, originally written with racist speech patterns and a character named Jim Crow, as dated and controversial. Positive: unconditional maternal love, that differences make you special, and that bullying is wrong.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 256, "t": "Bambi", "y": "1942", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Disney's original circle-of-life story.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: hunters fatally shoot Bambi's mother off-screen, and the film has other intense sequences -- a raging forest fire, a thunderstorm, a quail shot out of the sky, and hunting dogs chasing terrified animals. No language or substance content. Positive: Bambi's mother models total selflessness, and the film builds strong themes of environmental stewardship, friendship, and community, portraying humans as a real threat to the forest.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 257, "t": "Saludos Amigos", "y": "1942", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "CSM reviews this together with The Three Caballeros as the \"Classic Caballeros Collection,\" calling them Walt Disney's love letter to 1940s Latin America, and recommends the pair for about age 4+. In this shorter, gentler travelogue, a caballero fires his guns into the air and a mail plane nearly crashes, but there's little else to flag. Positive: CSM highlights that, for the era, the film's genuine celebration of Latin American art, music, dance, and tradition is a pleasant surprise rather than caricature.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 258, "t": "The Three Caballeros", "y": "1944", "la": "6+", "ca": "4+", "full": "Reviewed by CSM alongside Saludos Amigos as the \"Classic Caballeros Collection\" (recommended about age 4+ for the pair), this segment is livelier and riskier: Donald gets drunk off a single cocktail, Jose Carioca smokes a cigar throughout, Donald and a llama nearly fall from a high bridge, and Donald repeatedly whistles at and ogles Latina women in a way that reads as dated. Dated song lyrics use period slang like \"three gay caballeros.\" Positive: as with its companion film, CSM credits it with real affection for Latin American culture, music, and art rather than mockery.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 259, "t": "Make Mine Music", "y": "1946", "la": "7+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "This 1940s Disney musical anthology does not have a Common Sense Media review, so this entry draws on Wikipedia and other sources. Concerns: the \"Martins and Coys\" feuding-families segment includes gunplay (edited out of some home releases), Peter and the Wolf involves wolf-hunting peril, and the finale has a whale harpooned and killed; the film carries general 1940s-era cultural stereotypes throughout. Positive: pursuing your dreams against the odds, romantic devotion, and artistic and musical expression across its ten short segments. Substitute assessment -- no direct CSM page exists for this title.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 260, "t": "Fun and Fancy Free", "y": "1947", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM recommends this Disney package film -- Bongo the circus bear and Mickey's Jack and the Beanstalk -- for about age 5+. Concerns: real peril throughout, including a much bigger, angrier bear chasing Bongo, lightning storms, cliff and waterfall dangers, and a giant who kidnaps Mickey and friends; one musical number oddly plays bears hitting each other as affection. Minimal language (\"stupid\"), no substances. Positive: Mickey's resourcefulness in rescuing his caged friends, Jiminy Cricket's sunny outlook, and the message to take life in stride rather than worrying about the future.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 261, "t": "Melody Time", "y": "1948", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Peril, outdated cultural references in '40s Disney cartoon.\" Recommended for about age 7+. Concerns are significant by modern standards: a heroic cowboy shoots up a town, Native Americans are portrayed as savages facing unprovoked violence, buzzards attack a dying colt, a baby is abandoned to be raised by coyotes, a smoking cowboy is framed as heroic, and women are largely shown as passive and appearance-focused. Positive: CSM credits the era's animation as innovative and spectacular even while noting its dated biases; largely a historical artifact rather than a straightforward pick for today's kids.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 262, "t": "The Adventures of Ichabod and Mr. Toad", "y": "1949", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Two classic stories told in the best Disney style.\" Recommended for about age 6+. Concerns: the Headless Horseman repeatedly attempts to behead Ichabod Crane with a sword, a sequence genuinely scary for sensitive kids since the horseman is never unmasked as the town bully; cartoon weasels are shown passed-out drunk with bottles nearby, and characters smoke cigars and pipes. CSM notes \"very little\" in the way of positive role modeling, critiquing how men scheme and compete to win a woman characterized as a flirt. Positive: an appreciation for two classic pieces of literature.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 263, "t": "Cinderella", "y": "1950", "la": "5+", "ca": "4+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Sweet fairy-tale classic has bullying, tense chase scenes.\" Recommended for about age 4+. Concerns: a clawed cat named Lucifer menacingly chases Gus and the other mice, Cinderella is pursued by shadowy steeds, and her stepfamily cruelly tears apart her ball gown; opening narration mentions a parent's death, and a king briefly smokes a cigar. Mild insults like \"stupid\" and \"pompous windbag.\" Positive: kindness, teamwork, and finding hope in hard circumstances, though CSM flags Cinderella's passivity and reliance on rescue as worth a conversation about gender roles.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 264, "t": "Alice in Wonderland", "y": "1951", "la": "6+", "ca": "4+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Surreal animated Disney classic with mild peril.\" Recommended for about age 4+. Concerns: Alice nearly drowns in a pool of her own tears, oysters are eaten by the Walrus and Carpenter, flowers throw Alice out of the garden, and the Queen of Hearts repeatedly shrieks \"Off with her head!\"; the Caterpillar smokes a hookah in every scene, and two other characters smoke pipes. Mild insults like \"stupidest,\" \"fat,\" and \"barbarian.\" Positive: Alice's curiosity and perseverance carry her through Wonderland, and she learns the value of actually following her own good advice.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 265, "t": "Peter Pan", "y": "1953", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Stereotypes and violence mar Disney adventure stalwart.\" Recommended for about age 7+. Concerns: very frequent peril, with Captain Hook pointing his hook and sword and firing guns at people, children kidnapped, threats of throat-slitting, and a forced walk of the plank; the film's offensive racial slurs (\"Red Man,\" \"savages,\" \"Injuns\") and dated gender stereotyping (\"girls talk too much!\") are significant concerns for modern viewers; kids smoke a ceremonial pipe, and adults drink. Positive: imagination and freedom are celebrated, but the film also stresses that maturity and responsibility matter -- though Peter tends to solve conflict with violence rather than negotiation.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 266, "t": "Lady and the Tramp", "y": "1955", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Classic Disney dogs paw their way into hearts of all ages.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: Lady is muzzled by an unkind human and chased by fierce dogs, a secondary character briefly appears to be killed, and the climax has a rat threatening a baby. CSM also flags the Siamese cats as racist caricatures of Asian people and other stereotypical portrayals worth discussing with kids. Positive: loyalty, being a good friend, protecting the vulnerable, standing up for what's right, and not judging others by their status.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 267, "t": "Sleeping Beauty", "y": "1959", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Disney classic is delightful but sometimes scary.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: an intense sword fight between the prince and a fire-breathing dragon (Maleficent transformed), the prince nearly falls from a cliff, Maleficent kidnaps and beats him (mostly shown in shadow) and rains boulders down via her goons; she also uses words like \"fools,\" \"idiots,\" and \"imbeciles\" and there's a passing reference to hell. Kings toast with wine and a minstrel drinks himself unconscious. Positive: love conquering evil, and both Aurora and Philip standing up for choosing who they marry; the good fairies model resourcefulness and loyalty.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 268, "t": "One Hundred and One Dalmatians", "y": "1961", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Lovable cartoon classic with cute dogs and a mean villain.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: Cruella De Vil can be genuinely frightening, the puppies are in real peril from her fur-coat scheme, her henchmen threaten the dogs, and there are tense high-speed chases; Cruella smokes cigarettes in nearly every scene, other adults drink, and Roger smokes a pipe. Cruella's insults include \"idiot\" and \"imbecile.\" Positive: the cruelty of greed and animal mistreatment, teamwork, and compassion, as the Dalmatian parents and their humans take in 84 additional puppies beyond their own litter.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 269, "t": "The Sword in the Stone", "y": "1963", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Delightful classic brings King Arthur legend to life.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: young Wart is often punished by his guardian and bullied by Kay, there's some peril during his and Merlin's magical adventures (including a spooky forest), and the witch Madam Mim can be a bit frightening; wine is served with meals. Very mild language (\"Blast me to Bermuda!\"). Positive: bullying doesn't make you a bully, the value of education, and that courage, love, and intelligence matter more than brute strength -- with young Arthur modeling strong character even before he becomes king.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 270, "t": "The Jungle Book", "y": "1967", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Jaunty animation, jazzy songs, iffy representation.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: the climactic battle between Baloo and Shere Khan isn't graphic but includes some upsetting moments, and a burning branch is tied to the tiger's tail as he flees; CSM specifically flags King Louie and the monkeys as drawing on racist minstrelsy imagery, worth a conversation with kids. Positive: friendship, responsibility, and finding family in unexpected places, with generous, caring characters succeeding while selfish or evil ones don't, and Mowgli's friends showing courage and teamwork to protect him.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 271, "t": "The Aristocats", "y": "1970", "la": "5+", "ca": "4+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Jazz and hijinks make this Disney cat tale meow.\" Recommended for about age 4+. Concerns: the butler Edgar spikes the cats' milk with sleeping pills and kidnaps and abandons Duchess and her kittens in the country, and a pitchfork chase creates brief injury scares; a goose gets comically drunk after a restaurant meal. CSM flags a Siamese cat character built on racist stereotypes, including exaggerated features. Positive: that friendship and family can form anywhere, loyalty, and the value of shared adventure -- with Duchess staying graceful through new experiences and the wrongdoing butler facing consequences.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 272, "t": "Robin Hood", "y": "1973", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Clever animated take on the folktale has some violence.\" Recommended for about age 5+. Concerns: frequent cartoon violence with characters fighting using bows, flaming arrows, swords, and axes, plus references to hanging and a noose; a character stumbles out of a tavern visibly drunk, and one disguise scene draws wolf whistles with suggestive costuming. Positive: Robin Hood and his Merry Men model empathy for the poor and stand against economic injustice, while the vain, greedy Prince John shows the costs of selfishness and poor self-control.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 273, "t": "The Many Adventures of Winnie the Pooh", "y": "1977", "la": "4+", "ca": "3+", "full": "\"Remains faithful to A.A. Milne's beloved classic stories.\" A few mild-peril moments — Pooh chased by a swarm of bees, Rabbit lost and disoriented in the woods, and a dream sequence where Pooh is shot at with a toy pop gun — might startle very sensitive kids, and Eeyore's gloomy self-criticism runs throughout. Positive: warm messages about friendship and looking out for one another, with an ensemble of characters modeling compassion and empathy.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 274, "t": "The Rescuers", "y": "1977", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Great adventure, but too dark for preschoolers.\" Concerns: a dark plot involving a kidnapped orphan girl held for ransom by the villainous Madame Medusa, who wields a shotgun; scenes of near-drowning and dangerous falls in the bayou; and an unsettling shot of a diamond glinting inside a human skull. Positive: teamwork, courage, and compassion, as the mouse heroes model helping those in need while greed gets its comeuppance.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 275, "t": "The Fox and the Hound", "y": "1981", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Heartwarming tale of friendship, but expect some peril.\" The film opens with the off-screen shooting death of Tod's mother, and later includes hunting imagery (traps, gunfire) and an intense bear attack that ends in a cliffside plunge. Positive: loyalty across social divides, as Tod and Copper's friendship survives the world's insistence that a fox and a hound must be enemies, plus a gentle message about turning grief into hope.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 276, "t": "The Black Cauldron", "y": "1985", "la": "8+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "This 1985 fantasy — Disney's first PG-rated animated feature — does not have a dedicated Common Sense Media review; this assessment draws on Wikipedia and contemporary reception instead. Concerns: the villainous Horned King seeks to raise an undead army from the title cauldron, test audiences and critics found the climactic \"cauldron-born\" sequence too intense for young kids (violent content was trimmed before release), and the Horned King has a memorably dark, skeletal design and is destroyed on-screen. Positive: courage and self-sacrifice, as the sidekick Gurgi gives his life to stop the cauldron's power, and Taran grows from a boastful pig-keeper into a genuine hero. Substitute assessment — no CSM page exists for this title.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 277, "t": "The Great Mouse Detective", "y": "1986", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Clever Sherlock Holmes tribute mixes wit, peril.\" Though rated G, the plot involves the kidnapping of a toymaker mouse and a scheme to kill the reigning Mouse Queen, carried out by the menacing Professor Ratigan and his creepy bat henchman; there's a pub brawl, a duel, and background drinking/smoking. Positive: intelligence over brute force, as Basil's cleverness defeats Ratigan's plotting, plus strong father-daughter devotion and teamwork between Basil and Dr. Dawson.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 278, "t": "Oliver & Company", "y": "1988", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Can't compete with Disney classics, but still fun.\" Loosely based on Oliver Twist, the film has some scary moments as the orphaned kitten Oliver falls in with a gang of street dogs and their down-on-their-luck owner Fagin, who owes money to a menacing loan shark. Positive: Oliver learns self-reliance, and the dogs model loyalty and mutual care for each other and their human friend, with the pack's makeup reflecting different NYC neighborhoods.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 279, "t": "The Little Mermaid", "y": "1989", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Superb, entertaining animated musical has some scary stuff.\" Concerns: a genuinely frightening climax where the sea witch Ursula grows to giant size and wields a trident, a chef who chases Sebastian with a cleaver, and a storyline in which Ariel trades away her voice for a chance at romance, worth a conversation about self-worth. Positive: Ariel models curiosity, adventurousness, and perseverance, and Sebastian's loyalty holds even when he disagrees with her choices.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 280, "t": "The Rescuers Down Under", "y": "1990", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"A rare sequel that improves on the original.\" Concerns: young Cody is threatened with being eaten by a crocodile more than once and spends much of the film in real jeopardy alongside a golden eagle and her eggs, and the villain McLeach is swept over a waterfall to his death. Positive: courage, teamwork, and helping those in need, with an anti-poaching, conservation message running throughout.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 281, "t": "Aladdin", "y": "1992", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"A magic carpet ride of a movie from Disney.\" Concerns: the sorcerer Jafar and his mind control can be scary for younger kids, chase scenes involve armed henchmen and mythological monsters, and the film leans on cultural stereotyping of its Middle Eastern setting alongside some potty humor and mild name-calling. Positive: love, loyalty, and honesty win out, and Aladdin's arc about not pretending to be someone else pairs with Jasmine's spirited independence.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 282, "t": "The Lion King", "y": "1994", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Musical king-of-the-beasts blockbuster is powerful, scary.\" Concerns: Mufasa's death in the wildebeest stampede is both sad and frightening, hyenas menace as a militaristic threat, there's an eerie elephant graveyard, and some reviewers flag villains drawing on Black, gay, and disabled stereotypes. Positive: family, sacrifice, and responsibility — Simba learns that running from your problems isn't the answer, and his parents model selfless love.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 283, "t": "Pocahontas", "y": "1995", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Fine for kids; just don't expect a history lesson.\" Concerns: three people are shot (one fatally), settlers use slurs like \"savages\" and \"heathens\" toward Indigenous characters, partial nudity during a swimming scene, and the film romanticizes a relationship between an adult Pocahontas and John Smith despite the real Pocahontas being a preteen. Positive: Pocahontas models bravery and moral courage as a peacemaker between two hostile groups, and her father Chief Powhatan is a caring leader who values her counsel.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 284, "t": "The Hunchback of Notre Dame", "y": "1996", "la": "9+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Touching but dark Disney tale cheers on the outcasts.\" Concerns: sword fights and an arrow injury, disturbing demonic imagery, and Judge Frollo's menacing pursuit of Esmeralda carries an uncomfortable undertone that goes over most kids' heads but not all parents'. Positive: it's okay to be different — Esmeralda fights for justice and defends outcasts, and Quasimodo's kindness and loyalty are rewarded; CSM recommends previewing before showing to kids under 9.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 285, "t": "Hercules", "y": "1997", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Lighthearted, but scary stuff is too much for littlest kids.\" Concerns: a lot of gore and intensity for a G-rated film, including the fanged, terrifying Hades, a hydra that gets decapitated, glimpses of dead and dying people in the underworld, and apocalyptic flood/fire imagery near the end. Positive: the film's real theme is that true heroism is about character, not fame, and Meg is written as tougher and braver than the usual damsel in distress.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 286, "t": "Mulan", "y": "1998", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Disneyfied but dignified tale of Chinese warrior.\" Concerns: scary, intense battle scenes with weapons and explosions, a genuinely menacing villain in Shan Yu, and the off-screen destruction of villages; gender stereotypes are played for laughs in ways worth discussing. Positive: perseverance, integrity, and courage, as Mulan works hard, stays honest, and challenges gender expectations to save her family and country.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 287, "t": "Tarzan", "y": "1999", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Kids will be drawn to Disney's fast-paced version.\" Concerns: a baby gorilla and Tarzan's parents are killed by a rampaging leopard, with aftermath scenes of grieving and bloody paw prints; there's also a burning ship, an animal rampage, gunfire, and a climactic villain death. Positive: acceptance across difference — \"hearts are the same\" — with Kala modeling devoted adoptive parenting and Jane written as a capable scientist rather than a damsel in distress.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 288, "t": "Fantasia 2000", "y": "1999", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Delightful, but may scare more sensitive kids.\" Concerns: dark, scary imagery paired with ominous music across several segments, including an overpowering jack-in-the-box with glaring eyes and sharp teeth, marauding rats, threatening eagles, raging fires and giant waves, plus a Penn & Teller segment involving an axe. Positive: music as storytelling, with several segments modeling heroism and perseverance, and an accessible introduction to symphonic music for kids.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 289, "t": "Dinosaur", "y": "2000", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Stunning, but can be dark and scary in places.\" Concerns: battles to the death between vicious predators and heroes frequently in danger, plus intense imagery — landslides, a meteor strike, tsunamis, cave collapses, and fire — heightened by spooky music and very dark settings; not for very young or easily frightened kids. Positive: teamwork and standing together, as the loyal, brave hero dinosaur risks himself for the less fortunate, modeling that the best leaders truly care about those who follow them.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 290, "t": "The Emperor's New Groove", "y": "2000", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Fun, goofy cartoon misrepresents Incan culture.\" Concerns: mostly comic peril, including a creepy nighttime jungle chase, and a sarcastic protagonist whose attitude could rub off on young viewers; despite the Incan setting, none of the principal voice cast is Latino, a real gap in representation. Positive: people can change — spoiled Emperor Kuzco learns humility and redemption through his friendship with Pacha, who keeps faith in him despite repeated mistreatment.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 291, "t": "Atlantis: The Lost Empire", "y": "2001", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Entertaining but intense adventure for tweens and up.\" Scarier and more violent than most Disney animated fare: mass-destruction sequences with tidal waves, a sea monster, and lava; extensive weapons use (guns, explosives) with numerous character deaths; betrayal among the expedition crew; and background smoking/drinking. Positive: CSM notes the positive messaging is thin here — Milo's genuine wish to preserve Atlantean culture stands out against companions driven by greed, though the ensemble does model multicultural teamwork.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 292, "t": "Lilo & Stitch", "y": "2002", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Great characters, life lessons in Hawaii-set Disney film.\" Concerns: sci-fi laser battles, gunfire, and explosions; a scene where a bullied Lilo punches and bites a classmate back; Stitch's fanged, destructive behavior; and insults like \"stupidhead\" and \"abomination\" played for laughs; Lilo and Nani's parents died in a car accident, referenced once. Positive: family can form in the most strained circumstances, and every character — even Stitch — gets a redemption arc built on compassion, courage, and perseverance.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 293, "t": "Treasure Planet", "y": "2002", "la": "7+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Outer-space pirate treasure hunt has fun, intense battles.\" This sci-fi spin on Treasure Island packs in near-constant action: ship chases, laser-blast battles, explosions, and falls, plus a loyal character's tragic death after a betrayal. Language stays mild (\"stupid,\" \"shut up,\" an exclamatory \"oh my God\"), and background drinking and a pipe-smoking character round out the concerns. Positive messages carry real weight, though: Jim Hawkins grows from reckless troublemaker into someone honest and brave, mentored by the morally complicated John Silver, while Captain Amelia models sharp, confident leadership.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 294, "t": "Brother Bear", "y": "2003", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Cute bear tale has some peril.\" A young man named Kenai kills a bear in anger after his brother is fatally mauled protecting him, and the spirits transform Kenai into a bear himself as a lesson — two character deaths and several tense, perilous scenes may unsettle sensitive kids, and the spiritual return of deceased loved ones could raise questions. There's light bathroom humor but nothing on language, substances, or sexual content. The story's real strength is its message of empathy: Kenai learns to see the world through another's eyes, make amends, and lean on family, with a talkative cub named Koda along for the journey.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 295, "t": "Home on the Range", "y": "2004", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Not a classic, but cute and fun for families.\" A trio of dairy cows sets out to catch an outlaw and save their farm from foreclosure, with mostly comic peril, a rollercoaster-style chase that might spook the youngest viewers, mild language, and a brief cross-dressing joke. No one actually gets hurt despite the cartoonish action. The film leans on wit and heart more than any explicit moral lesson, with its animal heroes working together and taking real initiative to protect their home.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 296, "t": "Chicken Little", "y": "2005", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Cute, sometimes-frantic movie has peril, potential scares.\" After Chicken Little's false alarm about the sky falling gets him mocked by his whole town, an actual alien invasion arrives, bringing pounding music, zapping alien machines, and a panicked-citizens opening that can run intense for younger kids; ongoing bullying and a bit of suggestive humor (including a scene of Chicken Little in his underwear) round out the concerns. Positive: the story rewards persistence and honesty, as Chicken Little is ultimately proven right, and his small circle of loyal friends stands by him even when the rest of town doesn't.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 297, "t": "Meet the Robinsons", "y": "2007", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Clever but sometimes scary time-travel adventure.\" Orphan inventor Lewis travels to the future and tangles with a villain whose robotic bowler hat wreaks havoc, plus a genuinely startling T-Rex chase and several comic-but-loud invention explosions. Language stays at G-rated taunts like \"booger breath,\" with a bit of coffee-drinking and an adult wearing what looks like a nicotine patch. Positive: Lewis keeps trying despite repeated rejection and refuses to hold a grudge against his rival, and the film's core message — that failure is just part of the path to success — is reinforced by its warm, oddball vision of family.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 298, "t": "Bolt", "y": "2008", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Super-dog adventure is fun, age-appropriate for kids.\" A TV-star dog who thinks his stunt powers are real sets off cross-country to save his person, running into peril mostly framed as scenes from his own show — explosions, a hostage situation, \"evil\" cats — plus a genuinely tense fire during the climax. Language is limited to mild insults like \"stupid,\" with some incidental Las Vegas product placement. Positive: Penny and the cynical cat Mittens both show real capability and courage, and Bolt's arc lands on the film's clear message that being loved matters more than having superpowers.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 299, "t": "The Princess and the Frog", "y": "2009", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"First African American Disney princess is a good role model.\" A menacing villain commands creepy shadow minions and casts voodoo spells, and a supporting character's death lands as genuinely intense for a kid-aimed movie, alongside cartoonish fights and some background champagne at parties. Language isn't a concern. Positive: hardworking waitress Tiana is Disney's first Black princess and a standout role model — resourceful and loyal, she saves herself rather than waiting to be rescued — though a few supporting characters carry some dated, underdeveloped stereotyping.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 300, "t": "Tangled", "y": "2010", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Fantastic princess adventure is fun, with great messages.\" Cartoonish chases, frying-pan bonks, and sword threats fill this Rapunzel retelling, and one character's death (plus a near-fatal knife wound for the hero) push past pure slapstick; language is mild (\"stupid,\" \"dumb\"), and a tavern scene plays a character's drunkenness for laughs kids likely won't clock. Heavy merchandising is also worth knowing about. Positive: Rapunzel is curious, brave, and self-rescuing rather than passive, and the film consistently rewards honesty, gratitude, and looking past appearances.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 301, "t": "Winnie the Pooh", "y": "2011", "la": "3+", "ca": "3+", "full": "\"Adorable big-screen Pooh adaptation is short and sweet.\" About as gentle as animated movies get: the only real wrinkle is the imagined \"Backson\" monster, whose description might unsettle the most sensitive tiny viewers, plus some harmless pratfalls and a fall into a pit. There's nothing to flag on language, romance, or substances. Positive: Pooh and friends model real teamwork and kindness, with Pooh learning to put others first, Piglet working through his fears, and Rabbit showing off his industrious side — a great pick for a child's very first movie.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 302, "t": "Big Hero 6", "y": "2014", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Awesome origin story is action-packed, deals with grief.\" A fire kills Hiro's beloved older brother Tadashi early on, and the film doesn't shy from the aftermath, pairing that loss with robot-combat action against a genuinely scary masked villain and one moment of possible self-sacrifice that plays as \"really upsetting.\" Language stays mild (\"nerd\" used affectionately), with some non-mean-spirited jokes about Baymax's rotund shape. Positive: the film treats grief honestly, showing that healing comes through leaning on the people who love you, while huggable healer-robot Baymax and Hiro's diverse, capable friend group model empathy, teamwork, and courage.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 303, "t": "Moana", "y": "2016", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Memorable musical with a Pacific Islander lead, some scares.\" Several genuinely dark, scary sequences punctuate this musical adventure — a lava monster's attack, a violent storm at sea, an enormous glowing crab, and coconut-shell pirates wielding weapons — alongside mild insults like \"sharkhead\" and a mostly-shirtless Maui. Positive: Moana is an outstanding role model, driven by curiosity, courage, and devotion to her people rather than any romance, and her influence pushes the once-selfish demigod Maui toward real personal growth.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 304, "t": "Ralph Breaks the Internet", "y": "2018", "la": "7+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Colorful, clever sequel tackles complexity of online world.\" A Grand Theft Auto-style racing game brings weapons and crashes, and an \"insecurity virus\" grows into a genuinely menacing monster amid looming deletion threats; language stays at affectionate name-calling and mild potty humor (\"stench breath,\" \"doody\"), with heavy real-brand product placement throughout. Positive: Ralph and Vanellope's friendship is put to a real test as their goals diverge, and the film thoughtfully weighs internet fame against what actually matters, modeling empathy, loyalty, and self-control along the way.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 305, "t": "Frozen II", "y": "2019", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Slightly darker sequel charms with catchy songs, messages.\" Battle flashbacks bring weapons and nongraphic references to past deaths, elemental spirits attack the group, and a stretch where Elsa appears frozen solid while Olaf melts away could genuinely upset younger kids (both are resolved happily). Language is essentially clean, romance stays at hugs and cheek kisses, and there's heavy merchandising to be aware of. Positive: the film leans into themes of sisterhood, courage, and accepting people from different backgrounds, with both Anna and Elsa modeling bravery and using their strengths to protect the people they love.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 306, "t": "Strange World", "y": "2022", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Sci-fi adventure/tender family drama has scares, peril.\" The Clade family's expedition into a hostile, unmapped world brings several life-or-death close calls (aircraft crash, ATV chase, on-foot danger), flamethrowers and weapons used against creature threats, and a presumed character death that isn't lingered on; there's also brief parental drinking and a sad stretch where a father walks away from his son mid-mission. Positive: the story centers on honest communication and repairing strained family bonds across generations, with supportive parents who accept their son as he is and a grandfather who owns his mistakes, alongside real environmental and diplomacy-over-conflict themes.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 307, "t": "Wish", "y": "2023", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Celebration of Disney animation is fun—but only just fine.\" The villain King Magnifico turns genuinely intimidating and uses his magic to hurt people in scenes that verge on torture-like, a heavy object gets pushed onto someone (no injury shown), and Asha's father's earlier death is discussed as an ongoing source of grief. Language is minimal. Positive: the film's central message is that your dreams are part of who you are and shouldn't be taken from you, with Asha modeling compassion, courage, and integrity while rallying her community toward teamwork and doing what's right.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 308, "t": "Moana 2", "y": "2024", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Teamwork, courage overcome danger in engaging sequel.\" Moana and her crew face several genuinely touch-and-go moments — a god-summoned storm with towering waves, a giant killer clam, poison darts from the Kakamora, and other close brushes with mortal danger — with only mild language (\"hate,\" \"crazy,\" \"butt\") and a single flirtatious wink as the extent of romantic content. Positive: Moana continues to model strong, devoted leadership as both sister and daughter, and the film's throughline about following your calling and persevering through setbacks comes through in genuine teamwork among the crew.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 309, "t": "Beauty and the Beast", "y": "2017", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Fantastic but scarier remake of the 'tale as old as time.'\" This live-action version turns up the intensity: a pack of snarling wolves attacks and leaves the Beast bloodied, Gaston shoots the Beast and whips the village into mob violence with torches and pitchforks, a character dies falling as the castle crumbles, and flashbacks show mothers dying of illness and children being separated from them. Language stays at mild insults (\"idiot,\" \"fool\"), with some kissing, cleavage in period dresses, and tavern drinking among villagers. Positive: Belle remains a smart, independent, book-loving heroine, and the story's core lesson — looking past appearances and class to see who someone really is — comes through clearly, alongside the Beast's arc toward humility and generosity.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 310, "t": "Monsters, Inc.", "y": "2001", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Cute, kid-friendly monster movie; may scare sensitive kids.\" Monsters who scare children for a living get an unlikely house guest in this comic-but-occasionally-tense film; its scariest stretch has the villain strapping a toddler into a machine built to harvest her screams, and a factory-wide panic breaks out over a child's sock. There's nothing to flag on language or other content. Positive: the film's clear message is that facing your fears pays off, with Sulley and Mike learning that making kids laugh beats scaring them, and the story rewards courage, compassion, and integrity while a corrupt authority figure faces real consequences for his choices.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 311, "t": "The Lion King 1½", "y": "2004", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Clever and mostly charming.\" Timon and Pumbaa narrate the events of the original from their own sidelines, so the hyenas' menace and predator danger return, along with slapstick violence like falls and near-misses on cliff edges. There's a heavy dose of gas, belching, and nose-picking jokes typical of the duo. Underneath the comedy, it explores friendship and what's lost when you abandon family to chase a carefree life alone.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 312, "t": "Peter Pan: Return to Never Land", "y": "2002", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Pleasant but forgettable sequel to Disney classic.\" Set during the WWII Blitz, it opens with real historical weight: bombs falling on London and children separated from their families. In Never Land, Jane faces mild peril and some scary moments from Captain Hook, though most of it plays for comedy rather than real threat. There's no profanity or objectionable content, and the film's clearest message is that girls can be just as brave and capable as boys, with Jane growing into a leader among the Lost Boys.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 313, "t": "Cinderella II: Dreams Come True", "y": "2002", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Sweet sequel has plenty of laughs for kids and parents.\" Told as three linked stories narrated by the Fairy Godmother and the mice, it's a gentle, low-conflict watch skewed toward girls, with a heavy layer of Disney Princess marketing around it. Cinderella models treating everyone as an equal regardless of rank, and staying true to herself rather than putting on airs now that she's royalty. Jaq the mouse gets his own spotlight adventure as a human for a stretch, which younger fans tend to love.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 314, "t": "Cinderella III: A Twist in Time", "y": "2007", "la": "5+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Cinderella uses brains, not magic, to win prince.\" The stepmother steals the Fairy Godmother's wand to rewrite history so her own daughter marries the Prince, and she's genuinely menacing in a few scenes, including a frightening carriage ride that puts Cinderella in real danger near a ravine. There's mild slapstick violence too, like a food fight and a cat that gets set on fire, transformed, and shrunk. On the positive side, Cinderella wins the day through her own courage and cleverness rather than magic, and the story also builds a warm, respectful father-son relationship on the side.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 315, "t": "The Little Mermaid II: Return to the Sea", "y": "2000", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"An uninspired rehash of the first movie.\" This one flips the original's setup: Ariel's daughter Melody is forbidden from the ocean after the sea witch Morgana tries to kidnap her as a baby, and years later defies her parents to swim anyway. Morgana closely echoes Ursula's menace and dangles baby Melody over a shark's open mouth in flashback, sharks later stalk Melody herself, and she nearly drowns in an undersea cave. The review flags real downsides in the messaging too, since Melody runs away after a fight with her mother and her parents deal with it by lying to her about her own heritage rather than being honest.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 316, "t": "The Little Mermaid: Ariel's Beginning", "y": "2008", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Through grief and peril, Ariel discovers music's magic.\" King Triton has banned music from the kingdom after Queen Athena is lost to pirates (it's left vague whether she's dead or captured, and the family mourns her), so this prequel leans on real grief and a temperamental, yelling father before Ariel finds an underground club that reawakens her love of song. There are chase scenes and peril along the way, plus some mermaid chatter about boys and kissing. Ariel comes across as adventurous, rebellious, and brave, and the story ultimately celebrates the joy that music can bring back to a grieving family.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 317, "t": "The Return of Jafar", "y": "1994", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"A genie that should have stayed in the bottle.\" This made-for-video sequel brings Jafar back for revenge after a bumbling thief accidentally frees him from his lamp, and the ongoing threat he poses may frighten younger kids. It's noticeably rougher around the edges than the original, and Robin Williams' absence as the Genie is a letdown longtime fans will notice. On the positive side, Aladdin is falsely accused during the story and has to stand firm on his principles, showing real courage and tenacity under pressure.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 318, "t": "Aladdin and the King of Thieves", "y": "1996", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"A reunion Aladdin fans will love.\" Robin Williams returns as the Genie for this one, and a band of sword-wielding bandits called the Forty Thieves crash Aladdin and Jasmine's wedding while hunting a treasure that turns everything to gold, leading to clashing swords, a rampaging elephant, and a fight to the death on a cliff's edge. The story also digs into heavier emotional territory as Aladdin confronts the father who abandoned him as a child. It's a lighter, less frightening villain than Jafar, but the mix of wedding chaos and abandonment themes plays a bit older than the original.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 319, "t": "Pocahontas II: Journey to a New World", "y": "1998", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"More romance than history, but kids will enjoy.\" Believing John Smith has died, Pocahontas travels to London to plead with the King on her people's behalf and falls for another Englishman along the way, with her animal friends providing comic mayhem throughout the journey. The film takes major liberties with real history, and there's a scene where characters (including a dog) get comically drunk. It's generally inoffensive family entertainment, though Common Sense Media notes it doesn't offer much in the way of clear positive messages or role models.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 320, "t": "Mulan II", "y": "2004", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Mediocre sequel has lightweight story; some peril, violence.\" Mulan and Shang are engaged when the emperor sends them, alongside three princesses being sent off for arranged marriages, on a mission that includes sword and trident fighting and a tense scene hanging from a rickety bridge. A jealous dragon character stirs up conflict, and there's a silly song about underwear. The film leans hard into its central message that love should be a personal choice made with the heart rather than a duty owed to family or country, though reviewers note the characters feel a bit more selfish and flighty than in the original.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 321, "t": "Tarzan II", "y": "2005", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Sequel offers an enjoyable jungle jaunt with young Tarzan.\" This prequel-style story looks back at Tarzan's boyhood among the gorillas, when he fears his human differences put his family at risk after a near-fatal accident and runs off on his own. Most of the roughhousing is slapstick, but one young gorilla shows a genuinely aggressive streak, breaking rocks against his own head and threatening to throw Tarzan off a cliff. It touches on real feelings of not belonging and fear of losing family, and the runaway Zugor, who starts out using trickery, eventually grows into someone willing to take responsibility for others.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 322, "t": "Lady and the Tramp II: Scamp's Adventure", "y": "2001", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Not as good as the original, but cute and fun.\" Lady and Tramp's rebellious son Scamp runs off to join a junkyard dog gang led by the genuinely menacing Reggie, which may unsettle younger viewers, and there's a tense dogfight and one mild use of \"butt\" as a punchline. Scamp gets disciplined by his father after breaking the rules, prompting real family conversations about why kids test boundaries and whether running away actually solves anything. The gang's charismatic leader ultimately gets exposed as a poor role model who loses friends over his bad attitude, reinforcing the story's message about appreciating home and being yourself.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 323, "t": "Bambi II", "y": "2006", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Bambi grows up in this sequel; some emotional intensity.\" Picking up right after his mother's death, Bambi is raised by his previously distant father, and the grief and loss from the original resurface, including a scene where a friend's mother is caught in a hunter's trap and frightening chases by hunting dogs. A rival fawn bullies Bambi over his name, and the Great Prince fights off dogs with his antlers in one intense scene. At its heart, though, it's about a father learning to open up emotionally and be present for his son, and about Bambi growing into patience and awareness of the woods' real dangers.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 324, "t": "The Fox and the Hound 2", "y": "2006", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Milder than the original and full of country music charm.\" A much gentler watch than its predecessor, this one has young Tod and Copper sneaking off to a county fair, with slapstick mishaps like a runaway Ferris wheel, a cow stampede, and a couple of accidental gunshots along the way, plus a close call involving a puppy and a truck. Bluegrass and country music fans will appreciate the genuinely strong soundtrack. The story emphasizes that friends can come from very different backgrounds, and models characters apologizing and making amends after they hurt each other.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 325, "t": "101 Dalmatians II: Patch's London Adventure", "y": "2003", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Entertaining animated sequel has some peril and violence.\" Patch teams up with a TV star dog to foil Cruella De Vil, who's now scheming to turn dogs into abstract art with the help of a beatnik painter, and there's real cartoon violence as she hits characters with objects and throws knives, plus dialogue about ways puppies could be killed. Cruella smokes constantly and there's alcohol at a gallery party scene. Underneath the slapstick, the story's message is that you don't need to look or sound impressive to be a hero, and that being true to yourself matters more than fitting in.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 326, "t": "Atlantis: Milo's Return", "y": "2003", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Long on action, short on storyline.\" Actually three linked adventures stitched into one movie, Milo and his crew travel the globe investigating mythological threats drawn from Norse gods to the biblical Leviathan, and some of the imagery gets intense for younger kids: hypnosis, spirits being summoned, apocalyptic \"end of days\" talk, and some pushing and head-butting between characters. The historical and mythological references are loosely sourced and not meant to be accurate. Kids will mostly be swept up in the chase scenes and action, and the throughline about light overcoming darkness gives it a straightforward moral center.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 327, "t": "Piglet's Big Movie", "y": "2003", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Not very interesting, imaginative, or exciting.\" Piglet feels forgotten by his friends and sets out to prove his worth, leading to mild cartoon peril: tumbles, falls, a scary waterfall, and a chase by angry bees, with Piglet himself getting stuck in a boot and dangling from a tree branch. There are a few brief sad moments that resolve quickly, and no real villain beyond the bees. The heart of the movie is that size doesn't determine importance, and that being helpful and appreciated matters, with all the Hundred Acre Wood characters staying loyal, honest, and kind throughout.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 328, "t": "Pooh's Heffalump Movie", "y": "2005", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Gentle story of friendship is fine for wee ones.\" When a mysterious bellowing noise convinces the Hundred Acre Wood gang that a scary \"Heffalump\" is nearby, young Roo is told he's too little to join the search, so he sneaks off on his own and gets separated and frightened along the way. There are mild falls and characters getting stuck, but nobody is hurt, and the \"monster\" turns out to be a friendly mother looking for her lost son. It's a soft, reassuring lesson about facing fears, not judging by appearances, and being kind to those who seem different.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 329, "t": "Tinker Bell", "y": "2008", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Sweet, simple story is best for young Tink fans.\" This origin story follows Tinker Bell discovering she's a tinker fairy and struggling to accept a talent that feels less glamorous than the nature fairies' gifts, with one scene of a hawk swooping menacingly at the fairies that could startle little ones. Expect heavy merchandising tie-ins throughout. Tinker Bell's arc as an inventor who'd rather build and fix things than follow traditional fairy expectations pushes back against girl stereotypes, and the story rewards curiosity, gratitude, and teamwork while a mean-spirited fairy faces real consequences for her behavior.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 330, "t": "Tinker Bell and the Lost Treasure", "y": "2009", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Gorgeously animated fairy fantasy teaches friendship lesson.\" Tinker Bell accidentally breaks the moonstone needed for fairy dust and, in a moment she isn't proud of, blames her friend Terence, setting off a journey through a dark, rat-infested shipwreck and past two initially scary (but ultimately silly) trolls. The friendship rift may be a little confusing for the youngest viewers before it resolves. The clear takeaway is about owning up to your mistakes and forgiving each other, with Tinker Bell showing real courage, creativity, and perseverance along the way.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 331, "t": "Tinker Bell and the Great Fairy Rescue", "y": "2010", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"A few tense scenes add drama to family and friendship tale.\" Tinker Bell gets captured by a fairy-believing young girl during a storm, and there are several tense stretches where she seems to be in real danger, including a threatening cat and a dramatic thunderstorm flight home, plus a scene where the girl's father wrongly accuses her of lying. It's balanced with plenty of humor so the tension never lingers. The story leans on \"faith, trust, and pixie dust,\" with Tinker Bell's curiosity and resourcefulness ultimately winning out and an emphasis on teamwork and imagination throughout.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 332, "t": "Secret of the Wings", "y": "2012", "la": "4+", "ca": "4+", "full": "\"Centers on sisterhood and family, with a wee bit of peril.\" Tinker Bell discovers she has a long-lost fairy sister, Periwinkle, living across the border in the frosty Winter Woods, but fairy law forbids the two lands from mixing. There are a few tense scenes where it looks like one sister's wings have been damaged, plus a sequence where the fairy lands start freezing over and threaten Pixie Hollow itself, which may worry the youngest viewers. The film centers on family, teamwork, and sisterhood, with both fairies choosing to defy the rules to stay connected to each other.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 333, "t": "The Pirate Fairy", "y": "2014", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Amusing pirate-themed Tinker Bell adventure; some violence.\" Tink's fairy friend Zarina grows frustrated collecting pixie dust without understanding it, so she steals some and joins a crew of pirates at Skull Rock, leading to more swordplay and hostage-taking than earlier Tinker Bell films, plus a baby crocodile that bites a character. No one is seriously hurt despite the increased action. As with the rest of the franchise, it circles back to friendship and teamwork, and gives Zarina a real arc toward redemption and forgiveness after her rule-breaking choices.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 334, "t": "Tinker Bell and the Legend of the NeverBeast", "y": "2014", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Exciting fairy adventure has loss, some scariness.\" This one centers on animal fairy Fawn, who befriends Gruff, a giant, lovable-but-genuinely-scary beast, while a militarized band of Scout Fairies hunts him down in intense chase and battle sequences. There are fierce storms and lightning, characters shown injured and unconscious, and real emotional weight around a bittersweet, separation-tinged ending, so it plays a bit heavier than other Tinker Bell entries. Its themes of curiosity, loyalty, and not judging by appearances come through clearly, with Fawn modeling compassion for a creature everyone else fears.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 335, "t": "Planes", "y": "2013", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Airborne adventure is OK for a few laughs; some stereotypes.\" Crop-duster Dusty Crophopper dreams of racing despite his fear of heights, and the film has some genuine peril around plane crashes and storms that could startle sensitive viewers, plus bullying insults like \"farm boy\" and \"loser\" aimed at Dusty. Common Sense Media also flags national stereotypes among the international racer characters and a mildly suggestive kiss-mark gag. On the plus side, Dusty's story is a straightforward one about not being limited by where you come from, backed by discipline, hard work, and loyal friends who stick by him.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 336, "t": "Planes: Fire & Rescue", "y": "2014", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Much-improved 3D sequel has some intense fire scenes.\" Dusty joins a team of firefighting aircraft and vehicles battling a raging wildfire, and the rescue sequences get genuinely tense, with characters trapped by fire, floodwater, and in a mine, plus a reference to past character deaths. There's some mild crude humor and a few gender stereotypes in how female characters are portrayed. At its core, the film is a tribute to the real sacrifices firefighters make, with strong messages about teamwork, putting others' safety first, and adapting when things don't go as planned.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 337, "t": "Treasure Island", "y": "1950", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "Disney's first all-live-action feature is more intense than modern PG fare suggests: pirates get shot and stabbed (one shooting shown in a painful close-up), young Jim Hawkins is wounded by a thrown knife and ultimately kills an attacker in self-defense, and drinking runs rampant among the sailors, with one drunk pirate falling overboard. Long John Silver is a genuinely dangerous antihero, a cutthroat who shows real affection for Jim, which muddies the story's line between virtue and greed. Positive: Jim's courage and integrity anchor the adventure, though sensitive kids may find the violence startling for a Disney classic.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 338, "t": "Old Yeller", "y": "1957", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"One of the best early Disney dramas,\" per Common Sense Media, and it earns that reputation with real stakes: the family dog fights off a rabid wolf and a wild hog (leaving Travis's father bloodied), and Travis ultimately has to shoot Yeller himself after the dog contracts rabies, a scene not softened for kids. Guns and hunting are a constant part of frontier life on screen. Positive: Travis grows into real responsibility running the ranch while his father is away, and the film treats grief honestly, showing that loving something worth losing is still worth it.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 339, "t": "The Shaggy Dog", "y": "1959", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"A lovable mutt of a classic comedy,\" per Common Sense Media -- the mild fantasy premise (a boy periodically transforms into an Old English sheepdog) plays for laughs, with violence limited to a shotgun fired to scare the dog off and no one hurt. The bigger sticking point today is dated gender roles: women are mostly background decoration, and there are broad stereotypes about Eastern Europeans. Positive: gentle, largely inoffensive family comedy for school-age kids, though the pacing can drag for modern viewers.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 340, "t": "Pollyanna", "y": "1960", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Wonderful family film with excellent messages,\" says Common Sense Media -- Pollyanna's relentless optimism wins over a whole cynical town, modeling empathy and gratitude in the face of real hardship. The one genuine scare is a fall from a tree that seriously injures Pollyanna, a moment that lands hard for young viewers even though it resolves hopefully. Positive: the film's whole point is the value of looking for the good in people and circumstances, with Pollyanna herself an unusually generous, other-focused role model.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 341, "t": "Swiss Family Robinson", "y": "1960", "la": "9+", "ca": "8+", "full": "A shipwrecked family builds an elaborate treehouse home and eventually fights off pirates in a wild, gadget-filled climax -- Common Sense Media notes the battle (sword fights, cannons, homemade booby traps) is bloodless but still looks and sounds real, with pirates killed by tiger, explosion, and trap. There's also a moment where the family's youngest son, about 10, is allowed a sip of his father's drink, plus some teen-boy jokes about women and dated Asian stereotypes in how the pirates are portrayed. Positive: the family's cooperation, gratitude, and resourcefulness under pressure are the throughline, even if some of the survival tips are more fun than realistic.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 342, "t": "The Absent-Minded Professor", "y": "1961", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"'60s classic is sweet, clever, and funny,\" per Common Sense Media -- the comedy leans on cartoonish pratfalls (head bonks, knockouts), a couple of head-on car crashes played for laughs with no injuries shown, and a chase scene involving gunfire. Positive: Professor Brainard refuses to bump a failing student's grade despite pressure from the football team, modeling integrity, and the film's larger message is that thinking differently -- \"marching to a different drummer\" -- is something to celebrate, not fix.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 343, "t": "In Search of the Castaways", "y": "1962", "la": "8+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "Not on Common Sense Media; this draws on Wikipedia and viewer parents' guides instead. Hayley Mills and Maurice Chevalier lead a globe-trotting search for a shipwrecked sea captain that piles on perils -- an earthquake, a flood that strands the group in a tree, a jaguar, a giant condor that carries off the youngest boy, and capture by hostile tribesmen -- all played as boisterous, old-fashioned adventure rather than real menace, though the sheer number of close calls can feel intense for younger kids. Positive: courage, loyalty, and problem-solving carry the group through every setback.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 344, "t": "Son of Flubber", "y": "1963", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Fun, harmless sequel to the Disney classic,\" says Common Sense Media -- the plot leans on a gun-happy neighbor, hints of marital friction and jealousy, and dangerous pranks, including flubber gas misused to fix a football game and a jealous character's stunts that put people at risk. CSM lists no specific positive messages here, but the inventive, do-it-yourself spirit of Professor Brainard's tinkering can spark kids' own curiosity about science and invention.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 345, "t": "The Three Lives of Thomasina", "y": "1964", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Cat-centric family classic touches on deep topics,\" per Common Sense Media -- a grieving veterinarian's daughter believes her beloved cat, Thomasina, has died, and the film doesn't shy from real grief and estrangement between father and daughter. Kids who've lost a pet or family member may find the cat's death-and-return storyline upsetting rather than comforting. Positive: the story is ultimately about honest emotional communication within a family and the healing power of love.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 346, "t": "That Darn Cat!", "y": "1965", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Amusing but long romp for both adults and kids,\" per Common Sense Media -- a Siamese cat leads an FBI agent to kidnappers holding a bank teller hostage, and though it's played for comedy, the kidnapping plot and the appearance of guns (no one is hurt) can unsettle younger or sensitive kids, and the runtime tests attention spans. Positive: Patti and Ingrid come across as independent, strong-willed young women, and the film credits following your instincts as genuinely useful.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 347, "t": "The Ugly Dachshund", "y": "1966", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Slow-moving story is for dog lovers only,\" per Common Sense Media -- an overgrown Great Dane raised alongside a litter of dachshund puppies causes constant household chaos, while two Asian handyman characters are played for racist stereotype-based laughs by today's standards. There's champagne at a party and a husband snaps \"shut up\" at his wife mid-argument (he apologizes). Positive: the mutual devotion between dog and owner is sweetly depicted, even if little else in the film holds up.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 348, "t": "The Happiest Millionaire", "y": "1967", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Some drinking in long, corny Disney musical,\" per Common Sense Media -- an extended tavern scene has two leads chugging mug after mug of beer and ends in a bar fight, plus champagne, wine, and constant cigar smoking elsewhere, all set against a lengthy 141-minute runtime and dated attitudes about gender and class. CSM flags no real positive role models here; the film's guiding idea is simply to seize the day, for better or worse.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 349, "t": "Blackbeard's Ghost", "y": "1968", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"A yo-ho-wholesome retread of Disney favorites,\" per Common Sense Media -- the ghost of the pirate Blackbeard haunts a mild-mannered track coach, drinking rum and getting into comical scuffles with an invisible foe rather than anything genuinely scary. More of a concern: the coach cheats and compromises his principles for a competitive edge, and there's careless gun handling, gambling, and reckless driving played for laughs. Positive: not much beyond harmless, old-fashioned slapstick fun.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 350, "t": "The Love Bug", "y": "1968", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"'60s comedy has dated stereotypes, drinking,\" per Common Sense Media -- Herbie the sentient VW Beetle helps a down-on-his-luck driver win race after race, but the film leans on broken-English Asian American stereotypes played for comedy, along with drinking, cigarette smoking, hippie drug jokes, and a scene where the car dangles from the Golden Gate Bridge in an apparent suicide gag. CSM flags no real positive role models; it's dated entertainment rather than a model of good behavior.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 351, "t": "The Computer Wore Tennis Shoes", "y": "1969", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"1960s Disney comedy has lesson on fame's price,\" per Common Sense Media -- after a lightning strike gives college student Dexter Riley a computer-like brain, mobsters with guns chase his friends in cartoonish, slapstick fashion (car chases, attempted abductions) rather than anything realistically scary. Positive: when overnight fame and luxury go to Dexter's head, he learns that everyone is out for himself in that world and chooses his real friends over status.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 352, "t": "$1,000,000 Duck", "y": "1971", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"'70s comedy about duck that lays golden eggs,\" per Common Sense Media (reviewed under its alternate title, The Million Dollar Duck) -- the main scare is a boy stuck on a breaking ladder several stories above the street, and the film leans on outdated gender stereotypes, showing women as financially incompetent and confined to domestic roles. The father becomes so fixated on the duck's cash value that he neglects his family, especially his hurt young son -- a cautionary example rather than a role model. Positive: the story's underlying point is that family matters more than money.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 353, "t": "Bedknobs and Broomsticks", "y": "1971", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Delightful Disney charmer may be too long for youngest,\" per Common Sense Media -- three children evacuated during WWII end up with an apprentice witch, and the backdrop includes Nazi invasion and London bombings, plus a lively animated/live-action battle finale with mild violence (a cartoon fish even smokes a cigar). There are some sexist remarks and outdated gender attitudes typical of the era, and thick British accents plus the 140-minute runtime can challenge younger kids. Positive: Miss Price is strong, confident, and kind to the children, and the film celebrates cooperation and standing up for causes bigger than yourself.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 354, "t": "Now You See Him, Now You Don't", "y": "1972", "la": "7+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "Not on Common Sense Media; this draws on Wikipedia and other sources instead. In this Dexter Riley sequel to The Computer Wore Tennis Shoes, a college student invents an invisibility formula that gets stolen by a paroled con man scheming to turn the school into a casino, and the ensuing bank-robbery caper and chase play out as broad, cartoonish slapstick rather than real menace. Positive: Dexter and his friends' teamwork and ingenuity save their college, echoing the earlier film's message that cleverness and integrity beat greed.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 355, "t": "Charley and the Angel", "y": "1973", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Wholesome, life-affirming family tale has some violence,\" per Common Sense Media -- a distracted 1930s father is told by a guardian angel that he'll die that night, and along the way there's a Chicago bootlegging subplot with gun-toting gangsters who threaten adults and even force boys into driving bootleg deliveries at gunpoint, plus a couple of near-fatal accidents. Positive: Charley transforms from a distant, grumpy father into one who truly appreciates his family, the film affirms that crime doesn't pay, and the daughter asserts her own choice in romance.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 356, "t": "Herbie Rides Again", "y": "1974", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Slapstick, friendly, heroic car returns in Love Bug sequel,\" per Common Sense Media -- a greedy developer tries to force a widow from her home, and along the way there's a drunk character slurring and stumbling, cartoonish slapstick violence (car chases, punching, building demolitions), a nightmare sequence where animated cars menace a man who falls from a skyscraper, and an insensitive Native American stereotype played as a gag. Positive: courageous Mrs. Steinmetz stands up to bullying and greed, and the film credits friendship, loyalty, and persistence with winning out.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 357, "t": "The Apple Dumpling Gang", "y": "1975", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Rollicking Old West ride lassos some stereotypes,\" per Common Sense Media -- a slick gambler is saddled with three orphaned kids who strike gold, and the comic Western trades in stereotypical Asian and Latino characters, along with slapstick falls and chases, drinking, and smoking. Positive: the film argues that abandoning kids is wrong and caring for them is admirable, though the ending's message that the female lead finds happiness only by giving up her career for homemaking reflects dated gender expectations.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 358, "t": "Escape to Witch Mountain", "y": "1975", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"They don't make them like this anymore,\" says Common Sense Media -- orphaned telepathic twins Tony and Tia are pursued by men who want to exploit their powers, with mildly scary chase and tension scenes (antagonists carry guns that are never fired) that could unsettle sensitive kids, but nothing graphic. Positive: the twins use their extraordinary abilities for good, and teamwork and perseverance ultimately get them home safely.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 359, "t": "Freaky Friday", "y": "1976", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Endearing '76 original may feel outdated to kids,\" per Common Sense Media -- a mother and teenage daughter swap bodies for a day, with comic driving mishaps and crashes played for laughs (no real injuries) and one secondary character's alcoholism referenced, though not shown on screen. Positive: living a day in each other's shoes builds real empathy between Annabel and her mother, and the film's whole engine is learning to appreciate what someone else deals with.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 360, "t": "The Shaggy D.A.", "y": "1976", "la": "7+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Dad turns into a dog in this vintage Disney movie,\" per Common Sense Media -- grown-up Wilby Daniels runs for district attorney while still cursed to turn into a sheepdog, and the sequel leans more adult than the original, with 1970s-style bar scenes (smoking, beer), corrupt criminals, theft, and mild peril, and only one real child character in the cast. Positive: the Daniels family pulls together to support Wilby's campaign, and the crooked gangsters ultimately face consequences.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 361, "t": "Pete's Dragon", "y": "1977", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Offbeat live-action/animation mix with lots of drinking,\" per Common Sense Media -- the seaside town's resident drunk, Lampie, is a running gag throughout, plus a villainous song joking about harming Pete, a teacher who hits a student, comic roughhousing, and some stereotyped supporting characters. Positive: Pete stays honest and kind and never turns to violence even when threatened, and his invisible dragon Elliott ultimately looks out for the whole town, with the film's throughline being hope and holding onto childlike innocence.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 362, "t": "Herbie Goes to Monte Carlo", "y": "1977", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Silly but fun French adventures of Herbie in love,\" per Common Sense Media -- Herbie falls for a Lancia during a Paris-to-Monte-Carlo road race, and the only real concern flagged is one mostly slapstick fight scene, with nothing notable on drinking, language, or romance content. Positive: CSM calls out a female mechanic character as an unusual example of a strong woman doing a traditionally male job, and the film is otherwise pure, harmless silliness.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 363, "t": "The Cat from Outer Space", "y": "1978", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Talking alien cat outsmarts lesser humans in '70s comedy,\" per Common Sense Media -- an extraterrestrial cat with telepathic and mind-control powers gets pursued by the U.S. military and a greedy schemer, with soldiers pointing guns at the scientist who befriends him and references to Cold War-era Soviet threats; the special effects and pacing feel dated to modern kids. Positive: the story's takeaway is that looks can be deceiving, with the unassuming cat proving smarter than the humans around him.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 364, "t": "Return from Witch Mountain", "y": "1978", "la": "8+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Creepy classic about mind control still provides thrills,\" per Common Sense Media -- in this Escape to Witch Mountain sequel, an evil scientist implants a mind-control device in young Tony, stripping away his ability to refuse commands, and injects him with sedatives more than once, which can be genuinely unsettling for kids. Positive: Tony and Tia's sibling bond stays strong throughout, and a group of streetwise kids help the siblings escape, modeling teamwork and looking out for one another.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 365, "t": "The Apple Dumpling Gang Rides Again", "y": "1979", "la": "7+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "Not on Common Sense Media; this draws on Wikipedia and other sources instead. Reformed outlaws Amos and Theodore try to go straight but keep stumbling into trouble -- an accidental bank robbery, an army supply-theft scheme -- in a broad comic Western full of pratfalls, punch-bowl brawls, and a flaming-fort finale, none of it played as real menace. Positive: the two bumbling leads are essentially good-hearted, and the film's slapstick chaos stays light and consequence-free throughout.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 366, "t": "Unidentified Flying Oddball", "y": "1979", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Disney time-travel fantasy adventure has mild peril,\" per Common Sense Media -- a NASA scientist and his android double land in King Arthur's Camelot, where a character is threatened with burning at the stake, there are sword fights with falls from heights, the robot loses limbs in battle, and a bikini-magazine image appears as a running gag. Positive: Tom uses his scientific know-how creatively and with integrity, and the film shows friendship forming across a huge gap in backgrounds and eras, with characters growing past their initial prejudices.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 367, "t": "Herbie Goes Bananas", "y": "1980", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"More adventures of the lovable Volkswagen Bug,\" per Common Sense Media -- Herbie tangles with a young Mexican pickpocket and Inca-treasure thieves aboard a cruise ship, and there's one genuinely upsetting scene where Herbie is thrown overboard, plus stereotyped depictions of Hispanic characters. Positive: the film centers on the growing friendship between Herbie and the pickpocket boy, and CSM calls it overall silly, harmless fun for young viewers.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 368, "t": "The Watcher in the Woods", "y": "1980", "la": "10+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"Dated Disney horror tale has occult themes, mild scares.\" An American family moves into an old English manor plagued by unexplained events tied to a decades-old disappearance; concerns include a girl thrown from a horse causing a car crash, a child nearly drowning, unsettling occult rituals, and a church fire. CSM notes the film offers little in the way of positive messages or fleshed-out characters -- it's mostly mood and mystery.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 369, "t": "Amy", "y": "1981", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "Disney TV movie about a compassionate teacher for deaf and blind children in the early 1900s. Amy leaves a controlling, unhappy marriage to teach speech to deaf students who face bullying from classmates and skepticism from adults who doubt they can learn to talk; the story also includes a child struck and killed by a train and another child's death from illness. Positive: perseverance and the message that disabilities don't limit what someone can achieve.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 370, "t": "Condorman", "y": "1981", "la": "7+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "No Common Sense Media review exists for this Disney spy-comedy about a mild-mannered comic-book artist who builds real gadgets and gets pulled into an actual Cold War spy mission. Per Wikipedia/IMDb parents' guides: mostly lighthearted, comic-style action with car chases and stunts, no real gun violence, blood, or strong language -- closer to a live-action cartoon than a thriller. Positive: ingenuity, courage, and an ordinary guy living out a hero fantasy. Substitute assessment -- no CSM page exists for this title.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 371, "t": "Night Crossing", "y": "1982", "la": "10+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "No Common Sense Media review exists for this Disney dramatization of two East German families' real 1979 escape to the West by homemade hot-air balloon. Per Wikipedia/IMDb: the film opens with a startlingly intense sequence for a Disney movie -- a teen tries to breach the border and is shot by an automatic gun, shown with visible blood -- and sustains Cold War tension (Stasi surveillance, arrests, fear of betrayal) throughout. Positive: courage, family loyalty, and the risks people take for freedom. Substitute assessment -- no CSM page exists for this title.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 372, "t": "Tex", "y": "1982", "la": "12+", "ca": "13+", "full": "\"A very frank and gritty story about two brothers.\" Mason and Tex fend largely for themselves while their rodeo-circuit father is away for long stretches; concerns include teen drinking and smoking, a friend's father dealing drugs, a police shooting, and candid talk about sex and pregnancy, including a girlfriend clearly stating her limits. Positive: responsibility, resilience, and learning to think beyond yourself despite a hard family situation.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 373, "t": "Trenchcoat", "y": "1983", "la": "9+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "No Common Sense Media review exists for this Disney mystery-comedy in which an aspiring novelist vacationing in Malta stumbles into a real conspiracy and murder plot. Per Wikipedia/IMDb: PG-level comic mystery/action with a mugging and a body count implied more than shown, plus mild romantic tension, but no explicit violence, language, or sexual content. Positive: curiosity, resourcefulness, and trusting your instincts. Substitute assessment -- no CSM page exists for this title.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 374, "t": "Something Wicked This Way Comes", "y": "1983", "la": "10+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"Book-based '80s movie about an evil carnival is scary.\" A sinister traveling carnival arrives in a small Midwestern town at night, offering to grant dark wishes while preying on residents; concerns include a decapitated head falling from the sky, kids fleeing through a graveyard in terror, a man tortured into a skeletal husk by electrocution, and a swarm of tarantulas attacking two boys, plus use of \"hell\" and \"damnation.\" Positive: that happiness can overcome evil, loyalty between friends, and forgiveness for a father who feels he's let his son down.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 375, "t": "Never Cry Wolf", "y": "1983", "la": "11+", "ca": "13+", "full": "\"Suspenseful, poignant wilderness film; a few gross scenes.\" A government biologist sent to the Arctic to study wolves faces real physical peril, including falling through ice and close encounters with the pack, and eats live mice in a gross-out experiment paralleling a wolf's diet; also includes brief non-sexual nudity and some pipe smoking and beer drinking. Positive: overcoming fear, questioning assumptions, and a strong environmental message about respecting wildlife.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 376, "t": "Splash", "y": "1984", "la": "12+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"'80s fishy fairy tale has sex, some cursing.\" Tom Hanks falls for a mysterious mermaid (Daryl Hannah) who appears in his life out of nowhere; concerns include brief nudity, sexual innuendo throughout, a character shown extremely drunk, a lecherous coworker who brings pornography to the office, and language like \"bitch\" and \"goddamn.\" CSM notes the central romance leans more on physical attraction than genuine connection, and finds little in the way of real positive role models.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 377, "t": "The Journey of Natty Gann", "y": "1985", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Compelling adventure too violent for some kids.\" Left behind during the Depression when her father heads west for work, Natty sets off alone to find him, riding the rails alongside other drifters; concerns include a man who picks her up hitchhiking and attempts to sexually assault her, a bloody dog fight, a train derailment, and vigilantes burning a shantytown, plus some smoking, drinking, and mild profanity. Positive: though Natty resorts to theft and other survival tactics, she finds real care and protection from unlikely companions, including a wolf and a young drifter.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 378, "t": "Return to Oz", "y": "1985", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"1985 follow-up to classic musical is tin-eared and creepy.\" Far darker than the original Wizard of Oz, this sequel has Dorothy institutionalized and threatened with electroshock therapy before she's swept back to a crumbling Oz menaced by a headless princess and a vengeful stone king; also includes a near-drowning and menacing creature chases. CSM warns kids 8 and under will likely find it disturbing. Positive: courage in the face of real fear, and loyalty in going to great lengths for friends, even when the adults around Dorothy dismiss her.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 379, "t": "Flight of the Navigator", "y": "1986", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"'80s time-travel adventure has some cursing.\" A 12-year-old is abducted by an alien ship and returns eight years later, having aged only a few hours, then escapes NASA custody in the spacecraft; concerns include surprisingly strong language for a Disney film (\"s--t,\" \"hell,\" \"bastard\"), a slur used about a dog, a stereotyped gag about tourists, a fat joke, and mild violence involving soldiers with machine guns. CSM notes the film is light on positive messages and role models overall.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 380, "t": "Benji the Hunted", "y": "1987", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Benji shines in another simple but engrossing dog tale.\" Swept overboard in a storm, Benji washes ashore in the wilderness, where he witnesses a cougar shot by a hunter and takes on the job of feeding, protecting, and sheltering her orphaned cubs against threats from the hunter, a wolf, an eagle, and a bear. Nearly wordless and animal-driven, with minimal human presence that younger kids may need help following. Positive: selfless heroism, responsibility, and compassion -- Benji even spares a frightened rabbit despite being hungry.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 381, "t": "Ernest Saves Christmas", "y": "1988", "la": "6+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Goofy holiday movie is OK fun for families, knowwhatImean?\" Jim Varney's Ernest P. Worrell picks up Santa Claus at the airport and ends up helping him find and convince a reluctant replacement, alongside a runaway teen; the plot's premise about swapping out Santa may confuse younger kids' sense of the legend, and there's heavy, brand-name product placement throughout. Positive: redemption and forgiveness, especially in the runaway teen's reconciliation with her family, and Ernest's well-meaning persistence in doing the right thing.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 382, "t": "Honey, I Shrunk the Kids", "y": "1989", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Silly but occasionally scary movie has lots of peril.\" An inventor accidentally shrinks his kids and two neighbor kids to ant size, and they must cross the backyard -- now a hostile jungle of monstrous insects -- to be found; concerns include a near-drowning that requires resuscitation, a harrowing run from a lawnmower, the death of a friendly ant, a father who speaks harshly to his son about sports, teens making out, and some parents smoking and drinking wine. Positive: bravery and teamwork among the kids, a father owning his mistakes and working to fix them, and a capable, resourceful girl who helps steer the group to safety.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 383, "t": "DuckTales the Movie: Treasure of the Lost Lamp", "y": "1990", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Ducks race a wizard for riches; mild thrills and spills.\" Scrooge McDuck and his nephews chase an ancient treasure and a genie's lamp beneath a desert pyramid while a shape-shifting evil wizard, Merlock, races to beat them to it; expect comic-book action -- tumbling buildings, a raging river, a burning bridge, and lots of narrow escapes -- that CSM says only the youngest or most sensitive kids will find scary. Positive: the family learns that a fortune isn't worth risking your life for, and shows real protectiveness and courage looking out for one another.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 384, "t": "The Rocketeer", "y": "1991", "la": "10+", "ca": "11+", "full": "\"More style than substance -- but fun, if gun-heavy, action.\" A stunt pilot who discovers a jet-pack becomes an unlikely hero when Nazi agents try to reclaim it, leading to car chases, aviation disasters, gunfights, and explosions, plus a mobster's menacing threat with a grill and a scene implying a kidnapped, drugged heroine in a compromising situation; mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell\"). Positive: the hero moves from chasing personal gain to real heroism, and supporting characters turn patriotic once they learn their employer is working with Nazis.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 385, "t": "Newsies", "y": "1992", "la": "9+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Kids go on strike in gritty, historical musical.\" Newsboys led by Jack Kelly organize a strike against a greedy publisher, facing adults who threaten to beat children with clubs and chains and real fistfights; kids and teens smoke cigarettes, a child drinks beer, and there's some language (\"damn,\" \"dumbasses\"). Positive: strong pro-union, pro-worker themes about collective action against corporate greed, perseverance, self-belief, and Jack's growth into a trustworthy leader.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 386, "t": "Honey, I Blew Up the Kid", "y": "1992", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Silly scientific mayhem.\" A sequel in which the inventor dad's experiment goes wrong again, this time growing his toddler son to giant size and sending him rampaging through Las Vegas; the film avoids realistic violence, sex, or profanity, but shows a genuinely out-of-control child, parents unable to control him, and reckless driving as adults scramble to catch up. Positive: mostly reassuring -- although one scientist cares more about his career than the endangered child, the other adults work together to keep the boy safe.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 387, "t": "Cool Runnings", "y": "1993", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Delightful underdog story of Jamaican Olympic bobsledders.\" Concerns include infrequent profanity (\"damn,\" \"hell,\" \"a--hole\"), several bobsled crashes, one cartoonish bar brawl with breaking bottles, and heavy product placement (Coca-Cola, Adidas, Red Stripe beer) alongside casual beer drinking. Positive: that outlandish dreams can come true through hard work, that integrity matters more than winning, and teamwork and perseverance overcoming prejudice.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 388, "t": "The Three Musketeers", "y": "1993", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Frothy '90s Disney remake offers light amusement.\" Concerns: sword-fighting deaths and one suicide by jumping, frequent sexual innuendo, cleavage and revealing costumes, kissing, a partial nude scene, drinking, and occasional mild language (\"damn\"). Positive: teamwork, honor, duty, and loyalty run through the story, though the swashbuckling heroes also lie and womanize even as they stay loyal to each other and their country.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 389, "t": "Homeward Bound: The Incredible Journey", "y": "1993", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Adventurous animal tale will have kids riveted.\" An uplifting, heartwarming trek with a few tense, scary moments as the three pets face bears and rushing rivers in peril; kids under 5 may not follow the plot, while older tweens might find the humor juvenile. Positive: family, teamwork, loyalty, courage, and perseverance, as the animals stay resourceful and devoted to one another the whole way home.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 390, "t": "The Santa Clause", "y": "1994", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Heartwarming holiday tale has potty humor, arguments.\" Concerns: the original Santa's fatal accidental fall, police drawing guns during an arrest, ongoing divorce-related conflict between Scott and his ex-wife, a slightly suggestive \"naughty or nice\" joke, \"hell,\" flatulence humor, and a vague reference to 1960s drug use. Positive: giving is framed as the true spirit of Christmas, and Scott's arc shows a selfish person growing into compassion, with Charlie's steady belief rewarded.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 391, "t": "Angels in the Outfield", "y": "1994", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Heartwarming movie about hope and baseball.\" Concerns: characters frequently smoke and one pours alcohol into coffee, comic slapstick violence including a bat hitting a character's face, occasional insults like \"idiot,\" and heavier themes of foster care and a friend's cancer diagnosis. Positive: teamwork and compassion bring about positive change, and unwavering optimism inspires even cynical people, as Roger's persistence and George's growth into a caring mentor show families and communities pulling together.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 392, "t": "The Jungle Book", "y": "1994", "la": "9+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "No dedicated Common Sense Media review exists for this live-action version, so this draws on Wikipedia and contemporary reviews. Shere Khan the tiger kills soldiers and Mowgli's father in the backstory, and later scenes involve deaths by quicksand, falls, and animal attacks that critics (including Roger Ebert) called unexpectedly intense for a PG family film; there's also a chaste adult romantic subplot between Mowgli and Kitty. Positive: courage, loyalty to one's chosen family, and standing up against greedy treasure hunters. Substitute assessment — treat the recommended age here as approximate.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 393, "t": "A Kid in King Arthur's Court", "y": "1995", "la": "7+", "ca": "Not on CSM; substitute source used", "full": "No dedicated Common Sense Media review exists for this title, so this draws on Wikipedia and general parents-guide sources. Content is mild: jousting and sword-combat sequences with no graphic violence, an opening earthquake that could startle younger viewers, and a chaste, age-appropriate crush between the two young leads. Positive: resourcefulness, courage, and using knowledge (and modern gadgets) to help others and stand up to a scheming villain. Substitute assessment — treat the recommended age here as approximate.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 394, "t": "Homeward Bound II: Lost in San Francisco", "y": "1996", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Definitely not as good as the first.\" No real violence, but enough mild peril to unsettle sensitive kids: the pets are nearly struck by a plane, one dog is feared killed by a truck, and aggressive dogfights and cigar-smoking dognapping villains (selling dogs to a lab) run through the plot. CSM notes no clear positive messages or role models in this sequel, unlike the original.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 395, "t": "101 Dalmatians", "y": "1996", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Engaging live-action canine caper has some peril, pratfalls.\" Glenn Close's Cruella De Vil is rarely without a cigarette and repeatedly demands the puppies be \"dog-napped and skinned\" for a fur coat; slapstick violence includes two thieves electrocuted on a fence, characters dragged into water by dogs, a purse-swinging assault, and a pitchfork menacing a hiding puppy. CSM notes the cartoonish characters offer no clear-cut positive messages or role models.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 396, "t": "James and the Giant Peach", "y": "1996", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Fabulous adaptation of Roald Dahl's classic book.\" James loses his parents to a charging rhino in the opening and is forced to live as a servant to his abusive, threatening aunts; later a many-toothed shark attacks during the ocean crossing. Positive: friendship, facing your fears, and finding people who love you as you are, with James modeling courage, curiosity, and teamwork alongside his insect friends.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 397, "t": "Flubber", "y": "1997", "la": "8+", "ca": "9+", "full": "CSM calls this a silly comedy many kids will enjoy, recommended for about age 9+. Concerns: a brief mild-nudity gag (Professor Brainard stumbles into a life-drawing class), nearly inaudible profanity, a sad scene where a beloved robot \"dies,\" and Flubber-coated balls used to bash the villains in a slapstick fight. CSM also flags the absent-minded-professor stereotype and a cast with little diversity.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 398, "t": "George of the Jungle", "y": "1997", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Live-action take on popular tale is family fun.\" Concerns: a mild George-and-Ursula romance with some sexual innuendo (jokes about \"Coffee, Tea, or Me\" and ape mating rituals), plus potty humor like flatulence and wedgie jokes. Positive: kindness and open-mindedness, with George, Ursula, and the jungle's native characters shown as more decent and intelligent than the condescending, exploitative \"civilized\" villains.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 399, "t": "Air Bud", "y": "1997", "la": "6+", "ca": "5+", "full": "CSM's tagline notes this \"sweet dog tale has some peril and mature themes,\" recommended for about age 5+. Josh is grieving his father's death, and the film depicts both animal abuse and a coach's physical abuse of a boy; mild language (\"sucks,\" \"hell\") and one adult drinking beer appear. Positive: teamwork over a win-at-all-costs mindset, responsible pet care, and Josh gaining confidence through caring for Buddy and joining the basketball team, with a resilient single mom and a mentor coach as role models.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 400, "t": "Meet the Deedles", "y": "1998", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Teen slacker comedy has some peril, potty humor.\" Broad, Wayne's-World-style humor includes crude terms (\"heinie,\" \"screw,\" \"fart\"), escaped-animal chaos, vehicle accidents and explosions played for laughs, and a couple kissing on screen. Positive: that easygoing, seemingly unmotivated people can still rise to responsibility, with one twin's science and computer skills coming in handy when it counts.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 401, "t": "My Favorite Martian", "y": "1999", "la": "9+", "ca": "8+", "full": "CSM's blunt tagline: \"A terrible movie -- beware,\" recommended for about age 8+. Concerns: crude, vulgar references (mostly euphemisms), potty humor, cartoonish violence, and a scene of non-alcoholic \"intoxication.\" CSM notes the film has no meaningful positive messages or role models, making it more filler than family classic.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 402, "t": "Inspector Gadget", "y": "1999", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Not nearly as good as the TV show.\" CSM's 10+ reflects relentless cartoon violence and a villain (RoboGadget) who threatens a child with knives and other weapons and burns down the town while shouting aggressively; heavy commercial tie-ins and one offensive Asian stereotype are also flagged. On the positive side, Gadget's niece Penny is called out as a strong, capable role model for girls.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 403, "t": "The Straight Story", "y": "1999", "la": "10+", "ca": "13+", "full": "CSM's tagline: \"Great, but young kids may be bored and restless,\" recommended for about age 13+ due to its slow, contemplative pace rather than any objectionable content — there's no strong language or nudity. Concerns: most characters smoke, and there are references to past alcohol abuse and an unwed pregnancy. Positive: the essential decency of ordinary people, close family bonds, and a thoughtful meditation on reconciling with an estranged sibling.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 404, "t": "102 Dalmatians", "y": "2000", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Sequel better than first live-action Dalmatians movie.\" Almost nonstop cartoon-style peril has puppies and dogs falling from high places, jumping onto a moving train, and threatened with knives, plus assorted human pratfalls; Cruella smokes in several scenes and there's a champagne toast, alongside mild insults and one \"hell.\" Positive: that actions speak louder than words — genuine change requires follow-through, not just declarations — with upstanding, honest, and brave heroes and a pointed critique of the fur trade.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 405, "t": "The Kid", "y": "2000", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Very funny and genuinely insightful family movie.\" CSM flags some rude, PG-level language, a schoolyard scuffle, a sad off-screen death, and a parent-child confrontation that may upset sensitive kids. The film digs into why insecure, hurting people can be unkind, giving families a real window into bullying dynamics. Positive: it favors honest self-reflection over a polished image, and shows a man reconnecting with his authentic, younger self.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 406, "t": "Remember the Titans", "y": "2000", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Inspiring football drama brings history to life.\" CSM flags real 1971 integration-era racism: angry crowds, slurs, segregated settings, fistfights, a brick thrown through a Black family's window, and a car accident that leaves a character paralyzed; football violence includes hard tackles and a broken wrist. Positive: empathy, integrity, and teamwork, as Coach Boone and Coach Yoast lead players across racial lines and team captains Julius and Gerry overcome prejudice to lead together.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 407, "t": "Recess: School's Out", "y": "2001", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Simply a TV episode blown up for the big screen.\" CSM notes the G rating undersells some vulgar humor, including T.J.'s crude bathroom jokes broadcast over the school PA and a gag referencing adult hygiene products, plus T.J. reading his sister's diary aloud without permission. Positive: a racially diverse cast with capable female characters, and the kids model loyalty, cooperation, and teamwork as they save summer vacation together.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 408, "t": "Snow Dogs", "y": "2002", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Comic action, appealing hero, splendid dogs; some scares.\" CSM flags extensive slapstick and cartoon-style pratfalls, a genuinely tense near-fatal cliff fall, an angry bear and a threatening dog, heavy product placement, and the emotional jolt of a man learning he was adopted. Positive: the hero models professionalism, honesty, and family devotion, and the film encourages openness to new people, places, and cultures.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 409, "t": "The Country Bears", "y": "2002", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"A dumb story about a bear adopted by humans.\" CSM flags a runaway-from-home plot where the bear hero doesn't tell his worried adoptive parents where he's going, an early deception about his adoption, a jealous sibling who cruelly reveals it, and a gag where a character plays music with his armpit that kids may imitate. Positive: the film explores how everyone feels different sometimes and encourages building connections with people (or bears) unlike yourself.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 410, "t": "The Rookie", "y": "2002", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Sweet, engaging true story more appealing to tweens and up.\" CSM flags a cold, unsympathetic father figure, family financial strain, references to divorce and remarriage, mild language (\"hell,\" \"damn\"), and adults casually drinking beer or wine after games without becoming intoxicated. Positive: perseverance and faith in a dream at any age, as Jim Morris models being an engaged teacher, attentive father, and loving husband who never gives up on his goals.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 411, "t": "Tuck Everlasting", "y": "2002", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Lovely version of the favorite middle-school book.\" CSM's 10+ reflects a kidnapping/abduction plot thread, characters shot at with rifles (they get up unharmed, being immortal) and a blow to the head, some drinking in a bar setting, and heavy themes of mortality. Positive: Angus Tuck's wisdom that you should fear an unlived life rather than death, plus Winnie's growing independence and the Tucks' hard-won kindness.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 412, "t": "Freaky Friday", "y": "2003", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Mother-daughter switch is fun comedic chaos.\" CSM flags occasional strong language (\"screwed,\" \"harlot\"), ableist insults like \"psycho\" and \"freak,\" romantic awkwardness once mother and daughter swap bodies, mild slapstick violence (an earthquake, a football tackle), and some dated stereotyping in a Chinese restaurant scene. Positive: empathy and communication, as both Tess and Anna learn self-control and gratitude by literally walking in each other's shoes.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 413, "t": "Pirates of the Caribbean: The Curse of the Black Pearl", "y": "2003", "la": "10+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"Rip-roaring fun for kids who don't mind skeletons.\" CSM's 12+ reflects sustained action violence and character deaths, a genuinely creepy undead pirate crew revealed in moonlight, alcohol throughout, mild profanity (\"bastard,\" \"damn,\" \"hell\"), and revealing costumes with mild sexual references. Positive: female characters who resist damsel-in-distress tropes and captain their own fate, though the roguish hero and other authority figures are morally murky and frequently deceive one another.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 414, "t": "Miracle", "y": "2004", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "A moving retelling of the 1980 US Olympic hockey team's gold-medal run. CSM flags frequent hockey violence (hard checks, on-ice fights, a bloody nosebleed), occasional profanity (\"candy-ass,\" \"bastard\"), beer at social gatherings, and players making sexual innuendos about women in the stands. Positive: perseverance and dedication, as Coach Herb Brooks's demanding training pushes his players toward an extraordinary, team-first victory.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 415, "t": "The Princess Diaries 2: Royal Engagement", "y": "2004", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"A fun romp for girls of all ages.\" CSM flags several romantic kisses, a spooky-music passageway scene, mild action sequences, and one champagne toast (no smoking or drugs). Positive: Mia navigates a law forcing her to marry before becoming queen with bravery, intelligence, and kindness, learning to balance following the rules with following her heart, laughing at her own mistakes along the way.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 416, "t": "Around the World in 80 Days", "y": "2004", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Book-based Jackie Chan adventure has cursing, innuendo.\" CSM's 10+ reflects nonstop slapstick and martial-arts action with many crotch-injury gags, mild profanity (\"bloody hell\"), crude bathroom humor, comedic drunkenness, a cross-dressing joke, and sexual innuendo. Positive: friendship, teamwork, and honesty, with women shown as just as brave, strong, and skilled as men in the film's fight sequences.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 417, "t": "Sky High", "y": "2005", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Cartoony high school superhero drama; OK for tweens.\" CSM flags knock-down, cartoonish superpowered violence (kids slammed through walls and windows), bullying including a literal dunking, a character who smokes, and high schoolers drinking at a party, plus mild language (\"butt,\" \"sucks\"). Positive: loyalty over popularity, as the hero learns his real worth has nothing to do with his social status or superpowers.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 418, "t": "The Pacifier", "y": "2005", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"A crass, crude, and overly-familiar comedy.\" CSM flags cartoonish action violence including an exploding helicopter and a glimpsed dead body, heavy potty humor (diaper jokes, a character drenched in sewage), crude language throughout, and racist and sexist stereotyping among the antagonists. CSM notes the film is thin on positive messages, leaning on stereotypes and clichés rather than real role models, though a Navy SEAL does grow to genuinely care for the grieving kids he's protecting.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 419, "t": "Herbie: Fully Loaded", "y": "2005", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Kids may enjoy it; most parents will snooze.\" CSM flags potentially disturbing demolition-derby and monster-truck imagery, mild sexual innuendo about Herbie's crush on a female VW, and heavy brand-name product placement. CSM notes the film is light on positive role modeling — the heroine lies to her father about racing, the villain cheats, and Herbie himself resorts to questionable tricks against rivals — though family loyalty and following your passion still come through.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 420, "t": "The Greatest Game Ever Played", "y": "2005", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Predictable but inspiring family sports movie.\" CSM flags family conflict (a father who opposes his son's golf ambitions), period-typical smoking (cigars, pipes, cigarettes), rowdy pub drinking scenes, mild teasing of a short caddy, a budding romantic interest, and one disturbing childhood flashback. Positive: perseverance and passion, as Francis Ouimet pursues golf against class prejudice with his mother's steady encouragement even as his father resists.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 421, "t": "Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead Man's Chest", "y": "2006", "la": "11+", "ca": "12+", "full": "Darker and scarier than the first film: Davy Jones is a genuinely nightmarish villain with a tentacled head and crab-claw hand, a severed beating heart sits in a box, and characters are stabbed, flogged, shot at, and nearly roasted alive over a fire. A giant sea-monster sequence plucks sailors off the deck, and there's talk of cannibals considering eating people (and a dog). Language is mild (\"damn,\" \"bloody\"), but innuendo is rampant throughout. Positive notes: Will and Elizabeth show real courage and loyalty, and even the selfish Jack Sparrow reveals a core of honor when it counts.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 422, "t": "Eight Below", "y": "2006", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Inspiring story about brave sled dogs; injuries, dog deaths\" is how Common Sense Media sums it up, and the peril is real: stranded huskies are attacked by a leopard seal and some die from cold and starvation, while their human companions suffer frostbite with visibly blackened fingers. There's a single use of \"s--t\" and one \"hell.\" It's genuinely distressing in places and may upset kids under nine, but the film's heart is loyalty, compassion, and refusing to give up on a friend, with Jerry's dogged (literally) perseverance the emotional center.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 423, "t": "The Shaggy Dog", "y": "2006", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "A silly, harmless remake that CSM says \"will entertain grade schoolers.\" Expect a lot of dog-related slapstick and butt-sniffing jokes, plus a cattle prod and a large needle used to threaten Tim Allen's character (who spends much of the movie turning into a dog himself); he also winds up accidentally unclothed outside a courthouse in a played-for-laughs moment. Language is mild (\"butt,\" \"pee\"), and there's noticeable product placement for Kellogg's and Coca-Cola. Underneath the chaos, a workaholic dad learns to reconnect with his family, and bad behavior gets real consequences.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 424, "t": "Invincible", "y": "2006", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "A fact-based underdog sports story CSM calls \"an inspiring movie about working hard and not giving up.\" The gritty 1970s South Philly setting brings considerable drinking and smoking, some profanity, hard-hitting football violence, and references to a character's death, reflecting a working-class world of scarce jobs and desperate times. Vince Papale's real-life journey from bartender to NFL walk-on shows how determination and self-belief can overcome long odds, even when others doubt him.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 425, "t": "Bridge to Terabithia", "y": "2007", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "CSM calls it a \"beautiful lesson in friendship for young and old,\" but it's genuinely sad: a main character dies suddenly in an accident, and the grief that follows is handled honestly rather than softened. A teen bully shoves and menaces younger kids, and one boy punches another in the face; the story also touches on loneliness and family and class tensions. Leslie is held up as a strong, imaginative, independent role model, and Jess's growth through their shared fantasy world of Terabithia is the emotional core -- a story about opening your heart to find magic in everyday life.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 426, "t": "Enchanted", "y": "2007", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "CSM's \"adorable fairy tale\" and \"fabulous family flick\" is gentle overall, though a wicked witch tries to poison Giselle, Edward duels a troll with his sword, and a dragon battle at the climax could startle sensitive younger kids. There's a modest shower/towel scene played for comedy, a character getting tipsy at a bar, and a few kisses including a \"true love's kiss.\" Amy Adams's relentlessly sunny Giselle wins parents over despite the naivete, and the film's real message -- that love doesn't have to look like a storybook prince to be real -- lands as a warm, gentle spoof of Disney's own princess formula.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 427, "t": "Underdog", "y": "2007", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "CSM's verdict -- \"not too many new tricks left for the old Underdog\" -- sums up this live-action update: cartoonish violence has characters dragged and hit with objects, a dog and a girl get tied up near a bomb, and a building is threatened with destruction, but nothing is graphic. Language stays mild (\"imbecile,\" \"idiot\"), with a little dog flirting and a human couple's kiss thrown in. Underdog's core message is using your powers to help others, and the story doubles as a bonding tale between a lonely boy and his widowed dad.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 428, "t": "National Treasure: Book of Secrets", "y": "2007", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "CSM flags this treasure-hunt sequel for frequent action violence: screeching car chases, shootouts, a flashback of a boy witnessing his father's death, and a staged reenactment of Lincoln's assassination, plus cave sequences with near-falls and rushing water that nearly drowns trapped characters. The review is notably blunt that the film offers few real positive role models, since Ben Gates and company repeatedly break laws -- including a plot to kidnap the president -- without much consequence. It's a fast-paced popcorn adventure more than a values lesson.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 429, "t": "College Road Trip", "y": "2008", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "A gentle, G-rated father-daughter comedy CSM says \"even young kids will like.\" Content stays mild: some \"stupid\" and \"dumb\" name-calling, brief hugging and kissing between the parents, teasing flirtation between teens, and broad slapstick (a police chief nearly falls out a window, a runaway pig wrecks a wedding, a taser gets used for laughs). The daughter lies to sneak off on her trip and the overprotective dad poses as a college student to follow her, both played for comedy rather than consequence. At heart it's a relatable, if exaggerated, story about a parent struggling to let a kid grow up.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 430, "t": "Bedtime Stories", "y": "2008", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "CSM notes Adam Sandler \"tames his jokes for family-friendly fantasy flick\" here, aiming squarely at kids rather than his usual crude comedy crowd. Expect only mild language (\"butt kiss\" is about as strong as it gets), some cartoonish gunplay in a Western-themed dream sequence, a brief clothed hot-tub scene with light flirting, and a passing reference to a character's party-guy reputation. The story's real engine is family: an uncle bonds with his niece and nephew while helping his sister, and the group teams up to save their school, modeling teamwork and perseverance.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 431, "t": "Race to Witch Mountain", "y": "2009", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "CSM calls this sci-fi reboot \"action packed and intense for a PG film,\" and warns it's \"more violent than most PG-rated Disney flicks\": explosions, gunfire, hand-to-hand fights, and car crashes make for loud, frightening confrontations, though there's no blood or visible death. Language stays mild, but characters do throw around lines like \"you're dead\" and \"deadly force authorized.\" On the positive side, Jack learns to trust the two mysterious kids he's protecting, a side character selflessly helps troubled kids, and the UFO-believing conspiracy theorists who get mocked early on turn out to be right all along.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 432, "t": "Old Dogs", "y": "2009", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "CSM's blunt verdict is \"clueless-dad comedy is forgettable family fluff.\" Two grown men get drunk on cocktails, prescription pills cause exaggerated comedic side effects, and there's plenty of slapstick falling and an Ultimate Frisbee mishap played for laughs; mild profanity and scatological humor round out the language, alongside flirting, kissing, an implied conception of twins, and a brief partial-nudity gag. Underneath the goofiness, Dan chooses time with his newly discovered kids over a big business deal, and the review highlights Dan and Charlie's decades-long friendship as a genuine model of loyalty, even as it flags the shakier message that the mom hid the twins from Dan for seven years.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 433, "t": "The Sorcerer's Apprentice", "y": "2010", "la": "8+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Kid-friendly thriller is fun, if lacking in real magic.\" The action is larger-than-life rather than realistic, with sword duels, electric blasts, and a few impalement-style scares that can rattle kids under 8. Language is very mild (a couple of \"hell\"/\"God\" exclamations), and there's little sex or substance content to worry about. Positive messages center on self-belief, with Balthazar as a principled mentor pushing hesitant Dave toward doing the right thing.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 434, "t": "Prince of Persia: The Sands of Time", "y": "2010", "la": "10+", "ca": "13+", "full": "\"Tepid video-game fantasy flick tame enough for young teens.\" Expect frequent chase-and-sword combat with on-screen deaths, plus a genuinely creepy hooded assassin squad; violence isn't gory but is near-constant. Language stays mild, and romance is limited to flirting and a couple of chaste kisses. The film's core message is that family loyalty should outweigh greed and envy, with the king modeling righteous leadership and Tamina holding her own alongside Dastan rather than just needing rescue.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 435, "t": "Secretariat", "y": "2010", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Inspiring true story about an amazing woman and her horse.\" Content is mild: a handful of \"hell,\" \"crap,\" and \"damn,\" some sexist pushback Penny Chenery faces as a housewife entering the male-dominated horse-racing world, and mentions of her parents' deaths that could sting sensitive kids. Slower stretches may test younger viewers' patience. The real draw is Penny herself, a determined role model who balances family life while proving she belongs in a world that doubts her.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 436, "t": "Tron: Legacy", "y": "2010", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Visually dazzling techie reboot has peril, fighting.\" Expect disc-weapon duels and motorcycle chases with near-death peril, mild profanity (\"hell,\" \"dammit\"), some flirting/kissing between characters in skintight bodysuits, and programs sipping bright cocktails from martini glasses. CSM flags the plot as confusing with thin characters, but the positive threads are there: Sam grows by taking responsibility for his father's company, Kevin owns his past mistakes, and Quorra models curiosity and self-sacrifice.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 437, "t": "Mars Needs Moms", "y": "2011", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Surprisingly touching adventure has sad moments amid comedy.\" The motion-capture animation makes tense scenes (chases, a near-execution, moments where Mom seems dead) feel more realistic than typical CG, which can hit younger or sensitive kids harder than expected. Insults like \"stupid\" and \"idiot\" pop up, plus mild flirting, but nothing worse. The heart of the film is genuinely sweet: it's about a kid learning to appreciate everything his fair, loving, selfless mom does for him, chores and discipline included.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 438, "t": "Prom", "y": "2011", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Unrealistic prom flick is bland for teens, fine for tweens.\" Compared to most teen movies, this one stays tame: dating and kissing but no sex talk or nudity, one character's admitted infidelity, a brief scuffle with a bloody chin, and mild language like \"oh my God.\" Two students also get chased by police after sneaking into a rival school. Positive threads include looking past reputations and stereotypes, choosing not to stay with someone unfaithful, and Nova's hardworking, compassionate example.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 439, "t": "The Muppets", "y": "2011", "la": "5+", "ca": "5+", "full": "\"Muppet comedy offers memorable laughs, musical numbers.\" A brawl breaks out during Animal's anger-management class, there's plenty of slapstick, mild words like \"idiot\" and \"oh God,\" some romantic subplot, and a stream of celebrity cameos and product placement. Underneath the gags, the film leans hard into teamwork and encouragement: Walter's fan enthusiasm helps him believe in himself, Kermit leads with optimism, and Gary learns to put others' needs ahead of his own.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 440, "t": "Chimpanzee", "y": "2012", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Chimp 'adoption' documentary has some scary moments.\" Young chimp Oscar's mother dies after a territorial clash with a rival group, and there's a tense leopard encounter during a thunderstorm plus a (non-graphic) monkey hunt that could upset sensitive kids. The heart of the film is Oscar's unlikely adoption by the group's alpha male, showing that love and care, not just biology, are what orphaned young ones need, alongside real cooperation within the chimp community.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 441, "t": "John Carter", "y": "2012", "la": "12+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"Mars-based action epic has strong fantasy violence.\" Battles get genuinely gory in places (decapitations, body piercing), a shape-shifting villain threatens planetary destruction, and a flashback reveals a character's wife and child's charred remains; there's also on-screen torture and death sentences. Language stays mild (\"hell,\" \"damn\") with brief drinking. On the positive side, John Carter grows from self-interest into selflessness, Dejah Thoris is a capable heroine who can defend herself, and the story champions alliance-building across different peoples.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 442, "t": "Frankenweenie", "y": "2012", "la": "8+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Burton's creepy young Frankenstein is perfect for tweens.\" True to Tim Burton's style, the black-and-white stop-motion film is dark and macabre: a beloved dog dies, reanimated pets and creatures terrorize the town, and scary imagery with suspenseful music runs throughout, which can be too much for sensitive kids under 9. On the positive side, Victor is shown as brave and loving toward his dog, Sparky, science and independent thinking are celebrated, and eccentric teacher Mr. Rzykruski encourages pursuing your own path.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 443, "t": "Oz the Great and Powerful", "y": "2013", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Colorful prequel is scarier, less magical than the original.\" This one skews darker than the 1939 classic: flying baboons that can be downright terrifying in 3-D, Glinda tortured by magical lightning, China Girl left with broken legs amid a destroyed China Town, and an intense twister with sharp flying debris. Oz himself starts out as a selfish womanizer who kisses four different women, though mild language is the only real language concern. The film's heart is believing in yourself and others, with Glinda's self-sacrifice and Oz's growth into a genuine protector.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 444, "t": "The Lone Ranger", "y": "2013", "la": "12+", "ca": "13+", "full": "\"Occasionally entertaining but overlong and overly violent.\" There's more violence here than the PG-13 rating might suggest, including a graphic cannibalism/heart-removal scene, references to violence against women, scalping, explosions, and a high body count shot fairly close-up. Despite the comedic tone, dark content runs throughout. On the positive side, the Reid brothers stand for justice, Tonto is driven to right wrongs done to his tribe, and Rebecca is written with real courage rather than as a damsel in distress.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 445, "t": "Million Dollar Arm", "y": "2014", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Feel-good sports dramedy is a fine pick for tweens.\" There's talk about J.B.'s sex life with some kissing and a morning-after scene, infrequent mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell,\" \"stupid\"), young characters getting sick drunk at a party, and heavy product placement. The film champions hard work, perseverance, and playing sports for love of the game rather than money, with J.B. growing from a self-interested agent into someone who genuinely cares for the athletes he represents, and Rinku and Dinesh showing real bravery chasing their dream.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 446, "t": "Bears", "y": "2014", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Some tense moments in Disney's nature documentary.\" Several sequences are genuinely nerve-wracking, including a hungry wolf and rival predator males stalking the cubs, plus a scene where a cub briefly vanishes and viewers may fear the worst (all the bears survive). The film centers on mother bear Sky's determined care for her cubs Scout and Amber, modeling themes of sticking together, protecting one another, and family devotion.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 447, "t": "Into the Woods", "y": "2014", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Sondheim's fairy tale musical is dark, complex, sublime.\" Central characters die and leave children parentless, a wolf preys on and eats a young girl (off-screen but implied), a witch may frighten younger kids, rampaging giants cause destruction, and there's depicted infidelity between married characters — none of it wraps up in a tidy happy ending. The payoff is a nuanced look at flawed but well-meaning people: choices have real consequences, family can take unexpected shapes, and bravery matters even when balanced against caring for others.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 448, "t": "McFarland, USA", "y": "2015", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Poignant story about Latino runners a winner for families.\" There's a knife fight that sends someone to the hospital, a possible suicide attempt, and a couple of instances of domestic aggression (a thrown beer bottle, a punched wall), plus themes around class and economic privilege. The story lands on strongly positive messages about teamwork and looking past stereotypes: Coach White, despite his flaws, helps his runners see their own potential, and the boys' hardworking, loving families are shown as the real source of strength.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 449, "t": "Tomorrowland", "y": "2015", "la": "10+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Visually exciting sci-fi adventure has unexpected violence.\" Robotic villains shoot and instantly disintegrate people in several scenes, and there are frequent explosions, chases, crashes, and some hand-to-hand combat, along with mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell\") and heavy product placement. The film's throughline is unapologetically hopeful: Casey's relentless optimism inspires others, Athena believes in her recruits, and Frank finds redemption by trading cynicism for purpose, all pushing the idea that dreamers and inventors can change the world if they keep going.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 450, "t": "Cinderella", "y": "2015", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Visually impressive fairy tale promotes kindness.\" Being live action makes it more intense than the animated original: Ella's mother and then her father die in drawn-out, emotional scenes, her stepmother and stepsisters are cruelly abusive (insults, neglect, locking her in the attic), and there are threats of poisoning and violence. Mild words like \"stupid\" and \"hussy\" appear. The film's guiding message, \"have courage and be kind,\" plays out through Ella's compassion and respect for others, though some reviewers note she's more often rescued than proactive.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 451, "t": "The Finest Hours", "y": "2016", "la": "10+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"Death-defying rescue comes alive in intense drama.\" Based on the Coast Guard's most famous small-boat rescue, this true story has frequent peril and frighteningly realistic natural violence, with boats breaking apart and being tossed by huge waves, and one crewman dies attempting to transfer between vessels. Language is mild (\"jackass,\" \"screw,\" \"hell\"). Positive messages center on Bernie Webber's honesty and willingness to risk his life for strangers, and Ray Sybert's calm, creative problem-solving under pressure that saves his crew.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 452, "t": "Alice Through the Looking Glass", "y": "2016", "la": "9+", "ca": "9+", "full": "\"Uneven, less-violent sequel has girl-empowerment theme.\" Calmer than the first film but still unsettling in places: Alice is briefly institutionalized and plunges a needle into a doctor's neck to escape, a storm-tossed ship endangers the crew, and there's a comically depicted death, plus insults like \"imbecile\" and \"bloody.\" Positive messages emphasize that a girl can do whatever she sets her mind to, with Alice shown as a courageous, determined sea captain who bucks convention, and the film underscores that family and friends who love you unconditionally are worth risking safety for.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 453, "t": "Pete's Dragon", "y": "2016", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Wonderful remake of Disney classic has peril, sad moments.\" This gentle remake opens with a car accident that kills Pete's parents, and later includes a wolf encounter, a gun pointed at the dragon, a tranquilizer shooting, and a fiery chase, all wrapped in frequent peril and a few sad, scary beats. Language is minimal, with only mildly crude jokes. Positive messages celebrate believing in magic, loyalty, and honesty, with Pete showing bravery and resourcefulness, and the story defining family as more than just biology while valuing forest and wildlife preservation.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 454, "t": "The BFG", "y": "2016", "la": "6+", "ca": "7+", "full": "\"Sweet Dahl book adaptation has big heart, big scares.\" Roald Dahl's dark tone survives the adaptation: Sophie is kidnapped by the BFG and hunted by other, much larger giants who eat children, a nightmare is planted in her mind, and drunk men are shown for comic effect alongside plenty of \"whizzpopping\" fart humor. Strong themes of courage, perseverance, and empathy run through the story, with Sophie and the BFG modeling genuine friendship, mutual protection, and the importance of not judging others by their appearance.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 455, "t": "Born in China", "y": "2016", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Nature docu has gorgeous vistas, moments of peril.\" This Disneynature documentary follows snow leopard, golden monkey, and giant panda families through breathtaking Chinese landscapes, but nature is unsentimental here: animals fight for dominance sometimes fatally, a hawk snatches a baby monkey, and a mother animal's death is shown, which can be upsetting for younger or sensitive viewers. Positive messages highlight courage and sacrifice through the snow leopard mother's fierce dedication to her cubs, family bonds, and perseverance in a harsh environment, with the panda mother patiently teaching her cub survival skills.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 456, "t": "Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales", "y": "2017", "la": "12+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"Talented cast can't save tired, action-heavy franchise.\" This installment keeps the body count high, with sword fights, drownings, a throat slitting, and creepy supernatural ghost villains, plus Jack Sparrow frequently drunk onscreen and mild profanity like \"damn\" and \"hell.\" Positive messages come through Carina, who embodies intelligence, logic, and curiosity as she pursues knowledge despite being dismissed for being a woman, and Henry, who shows selflessness and bravery trying to save his father, with the story valuing unconditional family love and teamwork.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 457, "t": "A Wrinkle in Time", "y": "2018", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Inclusive but imperfect take on classic story of hope, love.\" The kids face genuinely perilous situations, including a twister and a frightening sequence where a possessed boy uses telekinesis to hurt his sister, along with some bullying and cruel comments, though profanity is minimal. Positive messages run deep: Meg, a brilliant young woman of color, leads as a scientifically gifted hero, and the guiding Mrs. Whatsit, Mrs. Who, and Mrs. Which model optimism, kindness, and courage, with the story centering on embracing your uniqueness, staying in the light, and the power of love and family.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 458, "t": "Christopher Robin", "y": "2018", "la": "6+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Sweet but slow tale about the power of family, imagination.\" Early scenes show Christopher Robin mourning his father's offscreen death and fighting in WWII, with guns and an explosion briefly seen, and a tense dream sequence along with anxious, monster-fearing animals (heffalumps and woozles) that younger kids might find unsettling. Positive messages emphasize the value of play, imagination, and work-life balance, as Christopher learns to prioritize his family over professional stress, and Pooh and friends model loyalty, compassion, and teamwork despite years apart.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 459, "t": "The Nutcracker and the Four Realms", "y": "2018", "la": "7+", "ca": "8+", "full": "Disney's visually lush but somewhat dark reimagining of the classic ballet leans into a creepy Mouse King made of swarming mice, sword combat, unsettling clowns, and peril-filled battle scenes, with a mother's death and ongoing family grief central to the plot, plus a single use of \"damned.\" Positive messages emphasize believing in yourself, using your talents, and persevering through mistakes, with Clara showing courage, cleverness, and selflessness as a girl passionate about science and invention, guided by a father and godfather who model handling grief and communicating through pain.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 460, "t": "Dumbo", "y": "2019", "la": "6+", "ca": "8+", "full": "Tim Burton's live-action remake is sweet but noticeably darker than the original, with a character crushed by a collapsing tent, threatening scenes, characters in peril from heights and fire, a spooky \"Nightmare Island\" attraction, and the sad separation of Dumbo from his mother, plus minimal language like \"hell\" and \"heck.\" Positive messages focus on treating animals right and not ridiculing others for how they look, with Mrs. Jumbo's fierce, protective maternal love and the human children's kindness and patience toward Dumbo reinforcing themes of empathy, compassion, and teamwork.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 461, "t": "Aladdin", "y": "2019", "la": "7+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Live-action remake has charming stars, intense moments.\" This version leans into a scary Cave of Wonders sequence with lava and pursuit, a confirmed death (a man pushed down a well), a drowning scene, armed guards, and a frightening full display of Jafar's sorcery, alongside very mild insults like \"street rat\" and \"riffraff.\" Positive messages emphasize looking beyond social class to see people's true character, with Aladdin growing from a thief into someone who stays true to himself, and an expanded, more empowered Jasmine shown as intelligent, empathetic, and capable of leadership.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 462, "t": "The Lion King", "y": "2019", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Visually stunning remake is darker, more violent.\" The photorealistic CGI intensifies everything from the original: lifelike scenes of animals hunting and fighting, a traumatic stampede that kills Mufasa, and genuinely creepy hyena antagonists, with mild language like \"stupid\" and \"pathetic.\" Positive messages center on family, responsibility, and sacrifice, with Mufasa modeling wise, compassionate leadership and Simba learning courage, accountability, and that a true king's power lies in compassion rather than force.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 463, "t": "Noelle", "y": "2019", "la": "5+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Christmas comedy has predictable storylines, positive messages.\" This gentle holiday film keeps things light, with only some out-of-control sleigh rides and chimney tumbles for peril, no profanity, and a passing reference that could raise questions about Santa's reality. Positive messages emphasize that Christmas is about giving rather than getting, encouraging kindness, self-belief, and following your own path, with characters extending forgiveness for mistakes and working to bring joy to others.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 464, "t": "Lady and the Tramp", "y": "2019", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Remake has humor and heart; some peril, emotional intensity.\" The realistic CGI makes this version's threats land harder than the animated original: dogs face danger from cars, rats, cats, and a menacing dog catcher, with Lady imprisoned in the pound, Tramp appearing unconscious after being thrown from a moving cart, and a rat pouncing on the baby's crib, alongside mild language like \"dang it\" and \"mangy mutt.\" Positive messages emphasize family loyalty and belonging, courage and teamwork in protecting loved ones, and not judging others by their appearance or background.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 465, "t": "Togo", "y": "2019", "la": "8+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"True Alaska tale mixes action and drama; some peril.\" Based on the true 1925 serum run, the film puts its sled dog team through life-threatening blizzards, near-cliff falls, and cracking ice, including a dog jumping through broken glass, with minor profanity (\"damn,\" \"Satan\") and some bar-scene drinking, plus the real backdrop of a diphtheria epidemic endangering children. Positive messages celebrate determination, courage, and selflessness, with musher Leonhard Seppala risking his life to save others and Togo himself embodying loyalty and persistence, while the Seppalas' marriage shows mutual kindness and support.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 466, "t": "Timmy Failure: Mistakes Were Made", "y": "2020", "la": "7+", "ca": "7+", "full": "A quirky, book-based misfit tale about a fifth grader who runs his own (imaginary) detective agency and refuses to see himself as anything but a genius. Content is mild: a fantasy car-crash-through-a-window daydream, a brief comic fistfight, and light insults like \"loser\" and \"dumb.\" A teacher and student face a tense moment on a bridge, and some adults treat Timmy unkindly, but a caring school counselor and his overworked single mom model patience and unconditional love. Positive messages about celebrating individuality, accepting help from caring adults, and learning self-awareness run throughout.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 467, "t": "Artemis Fowl", "y": "2020", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "A dense fantasy adaptation about a 12-year-old criminal-mastermind-in-training whose father goes missing. Expect frequent peril: a hostage situation with Artemis's father held in genuinely frightening captivity, a giant troll rampage that nearly kills several characters, fairy military battles, and fire-wielding goblins; one character appears to die before being revived by magic. Language stays mild (insults like \"traitor\" and \"thief\"), but the missing-parent plot and intense action may unsettle younger kids. Positive messages emphasize that people are complicated and shouldn't be judged solely by their parents' choices, with Artemis's cleverness, Holly's bravery, and the Butlers' loyalty offered as models, even as the Fowls' own illegal schemes complicate the picture.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 468, "t": "Safety", "y": "2020", "la": "10+", "ca": "11+", "full": "\"Emotional, intense, inspiring true tale of family, teamwork,\" as Common Sense Media puts it, based on the real story of Clemson football player Ray McElrathbey secretly raising his younger brother on campus. Heavier than typical Disney fare: their mother struggles with drug dependency and cycles through prison and rehab, there are scenes of family separation and Ray's angry outbursts, college students drink at parties, and mild language (\"damn,\" \"hell\") appears. Positive messages about sacrifice, asking for help, and how teammates and coaches can become family carry the film, with Ray's determination to keep his brother safe while chasing his own future as the clear takeaway.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 469, "t": "Godmothered", "y": "2020", "la": "7+", "ca": "8+", "full": "A modern, comic riff on Cinderella following Eleanor, a bumbling fairy-godmother-in-training determined to prove happy endings still matter. Silly fairy-tale-style profanity substitutes (\"mother of fudge,\" \"son of a butterscotch\") and mild insults like \"weirdo\" fill the dialogue, along with a bar scene where adults drink, a couple of innuendo-laden jokes likely to sail over kids' heads, and a reference to a father's death in a car accident. The film's real message pushes back against fairy-tale convention: true love comes in many forms, and a full life doesn't require a storybook romance, with Eleanor's earnest kindness and the grieving family's mutual support as the emotional center.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 470, "t": "Elephant", "y": "2020", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "This Disneynature documentary follows elephant matriarch Gaia leading her herd, including young Jomo, across the Kalahari in search of water. Common Sense Media calls out real peril along the way: a baby elephant gets stuck in thick mud, a river crossing turns frightening, and predators like crocodiles, lions, and hyenas threaten the herd; near the end, an elderly matriarch dies of natural causes in a tender but sad scene, with the possibility her body may be scavenged afterward. The film's heart is family loyalty, showing elephants as emotionally intelligent animals whose herd \"never leaves a member behind,\" alongside a strong conservation message about protecting the ecosystems they depend on.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 471, "t": "Dolphin Reef", "y": "2020", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "Narrated by Natalie Portman, this Disneynature documentary follows a young dolphin named Echo exploring his coral reef home. Two sequences bring real tension: a dolphin calf gets separated from his mother and pod overnight, and a mother-and-calf humpback whale pair is chased by hunting orcas before other whales intervene to protect them; the film also shows the ocean food chain plainly, including creatures being caught and eaten. Otherwise gentle and soothing, it emphasizes family bonds, teamwork, and how adult dolphins and whales teach and protect their young, framing every creature, however small, as essential to the reef's balance.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 472, "t": "Cruella", "y": "2021", "la": "11+", "ca": "12+", "full": "\"De Vilishly delightful, daring, and dark,\" as Common Sense Media describes this villain origin story. Estella's mother is killed on screen, attack dogs menace characters, and the film includes childhood bullying and cruel, abusive behavior woven through its revenge plot; \"hell\" appears on signage, mild words like \"stupid\" and \"psycho\" pop up, and one scene implies drunkenness at a party. Positive messages about friendship, teamwork, and resilience come through Estella's fearless determination and her loyal friends Jasper and Horace, and the film updates the classic with more racial diversity and LGBTQ+ representation than the 1961 original, though parents may want to discuss its more morally ambiguous heroine.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 473, "t": "Jungle Cruise", "y": "2021", "la": "10+", "ca": "11+", "full": "A ride-inspired adventure that's \"fun, if predictable,\" with frequent peril: undead conquistadores made of bees and snakes, dangerous encounters with piranhas and a jaguar, and guns, swords, and knives in near-constant use. Adults drink throughout (flasks, taverns), the leads flirt and share a few kisses, and an early stereotypical depiction of Amazonian tribespeople as cannibals is later subverted. Language stays mild. The film promotes courage, perseverance, and teamwork, with Dr. Lily Houghton standing out as a pioneering, unapologetically bold female scientist who refuses to shrink herself to fit expectations.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 474, "t": "Pinocchio", "y": "2022", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "This live-action/CGI retelling leans darker and more intense than the animated original, with frequent peril: Pinocchio is kidnapped, imprisoned, nearly turned into a donkey, and swallowed by a giant sea monster, while a beloved character appears to die and others are threatened, set on fire, or enslaved along the way. Mild insults like \"jackass,\" \"stupid,\" and \"loser\" appear, and some sequences may frighten or confuse younger viewers. The story's core message is about developing a conscience and telling right from wrong, with Geppetto's unconditional love and Jiminy Cricket's ethical guidance helping Pinocchio earn honesty, bravery, and self-control.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 475, "t": "Hocus Pocus 2", "y": "2022", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Campy but entertaining,\" with the Sanderson sisters back to threaten teenagers, cast spells involving blood and bones, and set fires, plus a played-for-laughs decapitated zombie. The witches openly discuss eating children to stay young, teens drink unsupervised at a party, and mild language (\"damn,\" \"idiot,\" \"nitwit\") rounds out the concerns. On the positive side, the film champions female friendship and empowerment, warning against choosing power over relationships, and its teen characters show real courage confronting danger to protect the people they love.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 476, "t": "Chip 'n Dale: Rescue Rangers", "y": "2022", "la": "7+", "ca": "6+", "full": "A live-action/CGI buddy reboot with a lot of cartoon-style violence: characters are tied up, slammed, shot, electrocuted, and frozen, and main characters appear to die and are mourned before it's revealed no one actually died. Menacing giant creatures threaten kids, mild language like \"hell\" and \"butthead\" pops up, and some jokes aim more at watching parents than kids. Positive messages celebrate loyal friendship over ambition, with Dale learning to value his old partnership and Chip letting go of a long-held grudge as both risk themselves to help each other and stop a black-market crime ring.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 477, "t": "Disenchanted", "y": "2022", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "This Enchanted sequel brings back Giselle for a lighter fairy-tale romp with mild scares: dragons, giants, a sleeping-potion curse, fires, and a wish gone wrong that briefly turns Giselle cruel and manipulative before she comes to her senses. Adults sip wine, teenagers flirt, a married couple kisses, and the villain Malvina is willing to put children in danger to hold onto power. The film's warmer message is that blended step-families are real families, nobody is perfect, and the real world can hold its own magic, with Giselle's underlying kindness and her daughter Morgan's courage anchoring the story.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 478, "t": "Polar Bear", "y": "2022", "la": "6+", "ca": "6+", "full": "\"Majestic footage, positive lessons, natural perils,\" as this Disneynature documentary follows a mother polar bear teaching her cubs to survive a changing Arctic. Nature is shown plainly: bears stalk and feed on prey including baby penguins and walruses, with graphic discussion of blood and blubber, a cub dies during a harsh winter, and male bears are shown preying on cubs. The film frames polar bears as resilient survivors passing hard-won knowledge across generations, with the devoted mother bear's teaching and protection of her cubs as the emotional throughline, alongside a clear message about climate change reshaping their world.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 479, "t": "Haunted Mansion", "y": "2023", "la": "10+", "ca": "11+", "full": "\"A spirited ride with peril, some scares,\" including jump scares, headless ghosts and skeletons, an ax-wielding ghost bride, a suggested suicide, an alligator chase, and a character struck by a car. Adult characters drink, sometimes to cope with depression, though language stays minimal (just one \"pissed off\"). Grief and loss run through the film honestly rather than exploitatively, landing on the message that grief can become \"a doorway to joy\" when faced together, with a diverse cast of characters modeling compassion, bravery, and teamwork as they support each other through loss.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 480, "t": "Peter Pan & Wendy", "y": "2023", "la": "9+", "ca": "10+", "full": "\"Captivating, moody adaptation has peril, some scares,\" as this darker, more atmospheric retelling leans into the weightier themes of Barrie's original: the psychological roots of Hook's rage, Peter's refusal to grow up, and Wendy's reluctance to leave childhood behind. Expect sword fights with Hook's crew, genuine peril for the Darling children, and a moodier visual tone than past Disney versions of the story. The film still lands on classic, hopeful messages about the value of imagination, courage, and family, while giving Wendy in particular more agency and depth than earlier adaptations.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 481, "t": "Mufasa: The Lion King", "y": "2024", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Well-acted but predictable\" origin story with real peril and violence: young Mufasa nearly drowns in a flood, the villain Kiros and his gang commit off-screen killings, and the climactic battle involves injuries and deaths that, while bloodless, land harder thanks to photorealistic animation. Kiros himself is drawn as genuinely scary and bloodthirsty, and a rift between brothers creates real emotional distress alongside a mild romantic subplot. The film's heart is found family, teamwork, and earning respect through action rather than birthright, with Mufasa's courage and compassion and Sarabi's boldness modeling how strength and empathy work together.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 482, "t": "Descendants: The Rise of Red", "y": "2024", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "This franchise entry sends Red, the Queen of Hearts' rebellious daughter, and Chloe back in time to try to fix their villain mothers' pasts. Content includes threats of \"beheadings and banishments,\" sword fights and chases, magical pranks that transform people into creatures, and psychologically abusive parenting; language stays mild (\"witch,\" \"tyrant,\" \"heck\"), and time-travel elements may confuse or unsettle younger viewers. Positive messages encourage treating others kindly (even adversaries), believing people deserve second chances, and letting kids grow into their own identities rather than their parents', with both leads learning to move past black-and-white thinking about who's good or bad.", "source": "DISNEY"}, {"num": 483, "t": "Snow White", "y": "2025", "la": "8+", "ca": "8+", "full": "\"Uneven, occasionally dark\" live-action musical remake in which the Evil Queen orders men to kill Snow White, menacing trees and creatures fill a frightening forest sequence, a character is shot with a crossbow, and Snow White's parents both die (one off-screen, one presumed). Mild language (\"shut it,\" \"fool\") and a budding romance with a few kisses round out the concerns, including one kiss whose consent Common Sense Media flags as ambiguous. The film firmly champions inner character over vanity, with Snow White's generosity, fearlessness, and empathy contrasted against the Evil Queen's greed and envy, and the diverse cast, including a wide range of ages and abilities, broadens who gets to be a hero.", "source": "DISNEY"}];
